[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from August 5, 2007 titled "Plite of the Single-Digit Millionaire."
[00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.000]   The cover story of today's Sunday New York Times is an article about millionaires in Silicon Valley who don't feel rich.
[00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.000]   Overall, I think the piece does an excellent job of capturing the local culture and feelings about money.
[00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.000]   However, I think it overlooks a few possibilities.
[00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.000]   Other than keeping up with the Joneses and analyzing Silicon Valley single-digit working-class millionaires,
[00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:32.000]   do they keep working because they are simply chasing those above them, as the article generally suggests?
[00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.000]   They truly enjoy what they're doing and want to keep doing it.
[00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.000]   They lack the creativity or boldness to step out of their workaday life and try something completely different.
[00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.000]   They are locked into a workaday life due to other constraints.
[00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.000]   For example, kids who they don't want to raise and bend.
[00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.000]   They are competitive, type.
[00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.000]   A people who view work as competitive and money as the score.
[00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.000]   While I think the story does a great job at portraying the outcome, seemingly at the expense of those who volunteered to be interviewed,
[00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.000]   think it does less well in assessing the reasons behind it.
[00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.000]   Some favorite quotes follow.
[00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.000]   "When speaking about the very real relative humility of many Silicon Valley millionaires,
[00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.000]   they recognized that if they happened to walk into a different office," said Marilyn Holland,
[00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.000]   a Menlo Park psychologist who has been counseling the Valley's elite for 25 years,
[00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.000]   "things would have turned out very differently."
[00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.000]   Then, when speaking on status in Silicon Valley, the founder of Match.com weighs in with,
[00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:27.000]   "You're nobody here at $10 million," Mr. Cremen said earnestly over a glass of Pinot Noir at an upscale wine bar.
