[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.640]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from September 18, 2007, titled "Fooled
[00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.940]   by Randomness." I read a great book a few flights ago, entitled "Fooled by Randomness,
[00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:15.120]   The Hidden Role of Chance in Mife and in the Markets" by Nassim Nicholas Taleb. The book
[00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.180]   was written by a mathematics and statistics adept trader/academic/philosopher who explores
[00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.920]   fun and interesting questions about probability, causation, psychology, and life. Simply put,
[00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:28.120]   if someone is successful in the financial markets, is it because they are skilled or
[00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.760]   lucky? Do they have a better strategy than someone else, or is their strategy than someone
[00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:36.000]   else, or is their strategy simply, accidentally more suited to given time period?
