[00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.080]   what happens when you sell all your revenue?" The not surprising answer, you end up a tiny
[00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:16.880]   company. Convera's two Qf38 results? $255,000 in revenues. Yes, that's measured in dollars,
[00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.360]   not kilodollars. Approximately the amount it would take to buy an Aston Martin V8 Vantage.
[00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.640]   A nice car, mind you, but a car. One car. They must set the record for smallest public company.
[00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:34.000]   A 225% growth rate, but off a tiny base. For perspective, if they can maintain a 225%
[00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.880]   growth rate, then in two years they'll be doing $3.5 million quarters and should still be a
[00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:46.080]   record small public company. One customer accounted for 92% of revenue, so they're actually one $235,000
[00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:52.240]   deal plus $20,000 company. Loss of $6 million in the quarter. I've heard one Convera customer
[00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:57.440]   describe them as "cheapest chips," you can see it in the numbers. They still have $54 million in
[00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:03.680]   cash, so they can keep losing $6 million/quart. Or for a long time, that is, nine quarters.
[00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.120]   It's hard for me to imagine that under these circumstances, they're a stable partner on
