[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from October 18, 2007 titled "Web2.0
[00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.800]   Summit, Evan Williams." I'd expected Sequoia's inimitable Mike Moritz and Microsoft's energetic
[00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.600]   Steve Ballmer to be my favorite speakers of the Web2.0 Summit, and they did not disappoint.
[00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.240]   Moritz provided a fascinating, cerebral discussion yesterday afternoon and Ballmer rocked the house
[00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.640]   this morning to see the CNN story on Ballmer's speech with this great quote regarding Live vs.
[00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:28.960]   Google. "You're just three years old and we've got you in there playing basketball with a 12-year-old,"
[00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.120]   Ballmer gushed and gesticulated, nearly popping out of his seat. "You're growing up quick and
[00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.720]   getting better every day and you've got all the potential in the world and it may take you till
[00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.720]   you're 7, 8, 9, or 10, but you're gonna dunk and you're gonna dunk on the other guy someday,
[00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:45.120]   Johnny." But the surprise for me thus far has been Evan Williams of Twitter, who, in a very brief
[00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.760]   presentation, had quite a bit to say on the merits of definition by removal and focus, instead of
[00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:55.520]   addition and napsion of drama. I want to discuss learning by the aggressive application of constraints.
[00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.000]   Our decision to use SMS as a messaging vehicle in Twitter meant that we had to support short
[00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.800]   40-character format, less messages. This was a huge constraint. We didn't define Twitter
[00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.840]   as blogger, less comments, tags, template editors, titles, etc., but it is an interesting way to look
[00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:15.360]   at it. We defined it as a ubiquitous friend status network. What else can we define by taking away?
[00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.600]   In my prior life at Blogger, I spent most of my time trying to add things. What would happen if
[00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.640]   you had Flickr without tags and with a one-photo/day limit? You'd get higher-quality photos and more and
[00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:29.200]   better comments on them, and you'd be photolog, which recently sold for $90 million. What would
[00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.160]   happen if you had Blogger without titles, tags, comments, and a 140-character limit? You'd get
[00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:38.320]   Twitter. What would happen if you had Yahoo, without the homepage, and just a blank screen,
[00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:43.200]   with a search box? You'd get Google. What would happen if you had MySpace, but you could only use
[00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.600]   it if you were in college? You'd get Facebook. This audio version was created by Grant Duncan
[00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:49.520]   and AI with permission from Dave.
