[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.160]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from October 22, 2007, titled "The End of an
[00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.880]   Architectural Era". I picked up via this post on the high-scalability blog a new paper by
[00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.520]   Michael Stonebreaker, Nabeel Hawkem, and Pat Halland entitled "The End of an Era It's Time
[00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:21.920]   for a Complete Rewrite" presented at the VLDB 2007 conference in Austria on September 23-27.
[00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.120]   From the paper's summary, as you know, I'm a big believer in the special-purpose DBMS meme.
[00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:31.280]   Any database historian knows what Codd was thinking, and more importantly, what he wasn't,
[00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:36.560]   when he designed the relational model. Again, from the paper, when he designed the relational model.
[00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.560]   Ted Codd's idea of normalizing data into flat tables has served our community well over the
[00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:45.120]   subsequent 30 years. However, there are now other markets whose needs must be considered.
