[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from November 8,
[00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.840]   2007 titled "Software Consolidation, Modern Conglomerates?"
[00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:11.600]   I had a long overdue lunch the other day with former business objects co-worker,
[00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.800]   and one of the smartest people I know, Alex Moises.
[00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.200]   While most people you talk to in Silicon Valley compare software industry evolution today to
[00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.240]   evolution of the automobile industry in the early 20th century, Alex had a different view.
[00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.080]   He thinks you should compare Oracle not to General Motors, but to ITT,
[00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.800]   that is, to the conglomerates of the 1970s.
[00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.720]   Conglomerates were built through acquisition, at sometimes pricey multiples.
[00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.280]   They did so largely for size's sake.
[00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.840]   Their leaders were incented to keep getting bigger.
[00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.640]   While there were arguably scale economies to be had, they were generally not realized,
[00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.240]   nor did they prove compelling compared to the disadvantages of the conglomerate model.
[00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.720]   In the end, they were largely broken up.
[00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:54.240]   It's an interesting viewpoint and well-grounded in reality.
[00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.640]   When I talk to my friends at the New Behemoths, I don't see any signs of any real product integration
[00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.880]   and/or discontinuation coming anytime soon, for example next five years.
[00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.960]   Nor do I see any obvious scale economies.
[00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.640]   In fact, when I talk to friends in two different divisions of Oracle,
[00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.640]   it's more like talking to people at diff, errant companies, than anything else.
[00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:16.720]   So, are we witnessing a consolidation, a lathe early automobile industry,
[00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.040]   or the growth of conglomerates a la ITT?
[00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.920]   My take is that while history never exactly repeats itself,
[00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:26.080]   that I would predict that a lot of products/companies do get spun back out of the behemoths
[00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:27.440]   before the movie ends.
[00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:31.360]   And you can't forget that the behemoths themselves are being disrupted at three levels,
[00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:32.800]   technologically by startups.
[00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.880]   The cost of being a behemoth is that you are so buried in integration roadmaps
[00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.160]   that innovation gets stalled.
[00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.720]   Price-wise by open source.
[00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:46.400]   MySQL, SugarCRM, Lucene, even Ingress all seem to be tipping away and moving up against
[00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.000]   their enterprise counterparts, business model-wise by SaaS and Google.
[00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.560]   Companies don't employ electricians today.
[00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.520]   Will they employ IT staffs to do basic operational systems?
[00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:58.800]   For example, HR, CRM, ERP tomorrow?
[00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:03.360]   Or will they just configure multi-tenant SaaS apps and focus their technology investments in R&D,
[00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:07.520]   as Jeffrey Moore would say, invest IT resource in core, not context?
[00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.800]   For more on the history of conglomerates, Alex directed me here.
