[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from April 1, 2008 titled "Clickview,
[00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.480]   A Return to Symbol." I've been hearing more and more from my old friends in the business
[00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:14.000]   intelligence, BI World, about a product called Clickview from a Swedish company, Clicktech,
[00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.600]   that was founded as a consultancy in 1993 and launched its product in 1997. Clicktech's
[00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.960]   war cry is one word, simplicity. And I like it. Most mainstream enterprise software packages have
[00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:30.320]   been around roughly 15 to 25 years. Over those years, two things invariably happen, the usual
[00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.880]   cycle of feature creep which, while well-intentioned, results in hard-to-use bloatware. Massive,
[00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:39.840]   multi-level market consolidation. When you take these two factors in combination, it's scary.
[00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:45.120]   Business Objects, for example, was founded in 1990, starting as a PC tools company.
[00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.400]   Then the web came along and we created a fairly separate web version of the product,
[00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:52.720]   Web Intelligence. Then enterprise reporting became important and, lacking the ability to
[00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:56.160]   build a nice enterprise reporting product, we bought our way out of the problem with the
[00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.520]   $1.2 billion acquisition of Crystal Decisions. Then, software consolidation started big time
[00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.160]   and the company I left in August for bought I guess a dozen or so additional companies/products
[00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:11.440]   such as a dozen or so additional companies/products such as Acta. Thus, when SAP bought
[00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:16.640]   business objects last year, they didn't buy one company/product. They bought literally dozens,
[00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.040]   each presumably with its own cycle of feature creep and none of them terribly well-integrated.
[00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.240]   And if you think that's scary, then think about the state of affairs at Oracle,
[00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.400]   who's been much, much more acquisitive. As I've ranted in this blog, I think the industry is
[00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.960]   starting to resemble the conglomerates of the 1960s. That leaves room for innovators of several
[00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:37.680]   types. Business Model Disruptors, people like Salesforce.com and the SaaS crowd,
[00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:43.440]   or people like MySQL, Alfresco, and the open-source types. Focus Disruptors, people who want to do
[00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.480]   one thing very well. Click tech is here, and I'd say that Mark Logic is as well. The Focus Disruptors
[00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:52.480]   offer a different value proposition than the modern enterprise software value proposition,
[00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:56.800]   which sadly has devolved to something like "I sometimes wonder on the personal front,
[00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.960]   when did we become our parents? At work, it's 'when did our industry become the mainframe
[00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.120]   business of the 1980s?' I'm a big believer that much of the backlash erupted from the
[00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:10.240]   1980s-era mainframe business, so will a backlash continue to erupt from the current enterprise
[00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:15.440]   software business. Open-source is part of it, so is SaaS. And I think focused best-of-breed
[00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.680]   product vendors will increasingly be part of it as well. It's working for ClickTech."
[00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:26.640]   In 2007, they say they grew 80% to $80 million in revenues and have 7,000,36 customers in 82
