[00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.760]   In restarting, I thought I'd blaze out of the gates with a controversial marketing rant on
[00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.640]   user conference branding provoked, in part, by a Stephen Arnold post on his Beyond Search blog
[00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.280]   about what I consider the disguising of Enstein's user conference. Stephen comes from a different
[00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:31.280]   place than I do. His focus is to question whether users should attend these topical,
[00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.360]   vendor-driven conferences or topical, vendor-driven conferences or topical,
[00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.800]   vendor-neutral ones. In some sense, I think he's taking the bait.
[00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.520]   Fact is, these supposedly topical conferences simply aren't. They're user conferences
[00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.080]   wearing wigs and lipstick, don't believe me? Then see the descriptive copy on Enstein's site,
[00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.000]   to create a unique user conference, for executives and technical developers.
[00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.480]   They buried it, but it's there. My question to marketing VPs is simple. When did user
[00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.320]   conference become a four-letter word? Why do marketing teams insist on dressing their
[00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.120]   user conferences up in wigs and lipstick? Examples.
[00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.720]   And because Discover Enstein's innovation leaders summit business objects.
[00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.600]   Insight, SAP's, Sapphire Cognos' performance. I have three problems with these photopical
[00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.320]   conferences. They're brand-elutive. Does Enstein really believe that people will say,
[00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.400]   "Hey Joe, are you going to the innovation leaders summit this year?" Sure, given enough size and
[00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.720]   money, you can actually achieve that goal. People really do say, "Are you going to Sapphire?"
[00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.400]   But even when you succeed, you fail because you've diluted your branding. What's more,
[00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.640]   if asked, "Hey Joe, what's Sapphire?" He'll say, "The SAP user conference."
[00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.320]   All you've done is to create a synonym, and where's the marketing value in that?
[00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:40.480]   And, sure as the marketing value in that. And, sure as the sun rises, marketing will
[00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.800]   print the conference brand on all those bags and... T-shirts in 10 times bigger type than
[00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.360]   the company brand. Heck, I've seen examples where they fail to print the company brand at all.
[00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.600]   They're misleading. A disguised user conference isn't a topical conference.
[00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:57.120]   If you went to the insight conference hoping to hear case studies of how people have used
[00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:02.000]   Cognos or MicroStrategy to gather insight from data, then you were sorely disappointed.
[00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.760]   If you're going to the innovation leaders summit, don't expect to hear how Elsevier, Oxford
