[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from May 29, 2008, titled " Coverage
[00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.520]   of CMO Club Panel Session."
[00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:11.000]   Go here to check out coverage of the recent panel session I did in New York City at the
[00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.280]   CMO Club Summit.
[00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:17.160]   Overall, I enjoyed participating on the panel and want to thank both Pete from the CMO Club
[00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.380]   for inviting me and the other panelists for the fun debate.
[00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:25.480]   From left to right, me Jarvis Cromwell, CMO of Storm Exchange, and Barry Hurstein, CMO
[00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:26.680]   of PayPal.
[00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:30.920]   The panel was about career management for CMOs and I was there to provide the "from CMO
[00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.440]   to CEO" angle.
[00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:36.800]   Not surprisingly, with a bunch of marketing people, I had trouble getting a word in edgewise.
[00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.640]   Here are some of the points that I made, which are covered in the blog post.
[00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.280]   Marketing is struggling for relevance.
[00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.360]   Product and sales groups are very powerful.
[00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.560]   Marketing gets relegated to a communications role.
[00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.080]   Present yourself as a business person, not a marketing person.
[00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.620]   Be with a successful company.
[00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.020]   People will ask you why you stayed so long with a losing company.
[00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:54.640]   Your job is to make sales easier.
[00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:55.640]   Don't be in the way.
[00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.720]   Surprisingly, it's very hard to get that alignment.
[00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.400]   Overall, I'd make a few additional points of advice to my fellow marketers.
[00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.040]   While I've been a CEO for four years, I nevertheless still consider myself a marketing
[00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.920]   guy and probably always will for four years and nevertheless still consider myself a marketing
[00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:09.920]   guy and probably always will.
[00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.700]   Talk less, listen more.
[00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.080]   We marketers are a chatty bunch and need to be self-aware.
[00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:20.160]   Align to the business, make this a never-ending quest, see point one for help in doing it.
[00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:23.800]   Remember that for the sales VP and the CEO, it really is all about the numbers.
[00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:28.540]   It's easy to preach about long-term investments, brand values, and other lofty things.
[00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:31.840]   Do so once in a while as it's your role, but do so with care.
[00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.160]   Choose your battles.
[00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:36.120]   Market for your sales force, not for other marketers.
[00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.280]   Marketing is a self-congratulatory discipline.
[00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.640]   Lots of campaigns that win awards don't move the sales needle an inch.
[00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:42.640]   IHE.
[00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.640]   I have one story from the panel that I think contains a rather pointed lesson.
[00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.240]   It goes like this.
[00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.800]   Audience member, I have a question.
[00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:54.160]   How many people in this room, panelists included, have ever carried a bag in sales?
[00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:56.940]   That is, had a sales job and sales quota.
[00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.800]   About 10% of the hands go up.
[00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.040]   Mine is not one of them.
[00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:01.860]   But I think the woman's begging a great question.
[00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:06.400]   Can you and how can you, if you've never walked a mile in their shoes, support sales?
[00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:07.400]   It's a great topic.
[00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:08.760]   I'm ready to riff on it.
[00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.640]   My favorite riff is about the poor salesperson who joins marketing or sales operations thinking
[00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:15.360]   sales will continue to see him/her as us.
[00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.280]   When unbeknownst to them, in the eyes of their former quota-carrying colleagues, they transition
[00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.320]   from us to them approximately 10 nanoseconds after renouncing their quota.
[00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:25.880]   I'm ready to go, thinking this is going to be great.
[00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:29.560]   Moderator, okay, now let's talk about the brand called me in managing your career.
[00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.320]   Me, wait a minute.
[00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.760]   You in the audience, where were you headed with that sales question?
[00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:38.020]   Audience member, I was thinking about how we can support sales if we've never done sales
[00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.560]   and that maybe people would be interested in discussing that.
[00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:41.840]   Sound of crickets chirping.
[00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.280]   Moderator, okay, now let's talk about the brand call me.
[00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:46.280]   Don't get me wrong.
[00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:49.440]   I'm the big believer in the whole "brand called me" thing and agree that people should
[00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:53.880]   treat themselves as marketable products from a resume perspective and establish clear positioning
[00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:54.880]   and differentiation.
[00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:56.640]   That's all great.
[00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.560]   But isn't there something wrong when a bunch of marketing people would rather talk about
[00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.160]   the brand called me than sales?
[00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:03.240]   Isn't that part of the problem?
[00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.800]   My closing comments on the panel were as follows.
[00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:10.960]   For career success as a marketer, first realize that as CMO you inherently have a dual role,
[00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:13.560]   head marketer, and e-staff contributor.
[00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.320]   Build a very strong team that you can basically leave alone to run marketing.
[00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:21.500]   Then put your effort into the e-staff contributor role to help you both develop as a businessperson
[00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.800]   and contribute to your company's success.
[00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:27.560]   And finally, I want to know why only me and the woman, dressed in black, want to talk
[00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:28.560]   about sales.
[00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.760]   She's my bet for the next CEO in the crowd.
[00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:32.920]   Related articles.
[00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:36.920]   Speaking at CMO Club Summit next week in NYC via Zymanta.
