[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.320]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from July 17, 2008 titled "Should my CEO have a
[00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:10.880]   ghost written blog?" I received this question the other day from an old friend. Clearly, having our
[00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.280]   CEO write his own real blog would be ideal, but do you think it's possible that a ghost written
[00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:19.360]   blog is better than nothing at all or is the downside not worth it? If you're against it,
[00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.760]   do you have any ideas on how to explain it to him and the marketing team pushing for it? And,
[00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.640]   and if you think it's doable, do you have any advice to him/the writers? My short answer is a
[00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:34.160]   vehement no. If your CEO is going to have a blog, then it should be his or her own. Why? Because,
[00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:39.040]   in a word, to do otherwise would be misleading. The promise of a blog is connection and interaction
[00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.360]   with the author on topics of shared interest. Readers expect blogs to actually be written by
[00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:49.280]   their stated authors. If the marketing/PR team writes the blog, it will, with all due respect,
[00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:54.000]   probably end up easily identified as marketing. Produced Pabulum, rephrasing and reinforcing
[00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.800]   company press releases. Odds are you can't bluff this, so you shouldn't try. Even if you have a
[00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.760]   highly talented and knowledgeable person write the blog, it will fail to capture the CEO's voice.
[00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:07.600]   When people meet me, they feel like they know me, and in a sense they actually do because of the
[00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.080]   blog. If the CEO simply wishes to air a few corporate thoughts every once in a while,
[00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:15.920]   you could accomplish that goal with a CEO corner in a corporate newsletter or on the company's
[00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.720]   website. If, on the other hand, the company wants to use a blog to comment on industry topics of
[00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:24.960]   interest that aren't necessarily appropriate for its own corporate website, then why not create a
[00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:29.680]   corporate blog, that is, the company X corporate blog? With this solution, you're not misleading
[00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.200]   the audience, they know they're reading a corporate blog, and you can make it a multi-contributor
[00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.920]   blog, and you can make it a multi-contributor blog where, perhaps everyon. See, in a while,
[00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:43.760]   the CEO weighs in. If, despite these arguments, you are hell-bent on a ghost-written CEO blog,
[00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:48.160]   then I do have this advice. Only write posts that are the direct result of live interviews
[00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:53.200]   with the CEO on topics that he or she chooses. Instruct the writer to suppress his or her own
[00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:58.480]   voice and instead work very hard to capture the voice of the CEO-O, in tone, in diction, and in
[00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:03.360]   style. Here's a slightly old article on the topic written by corporate and CEO blogging expert,
[00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:07.520]   Debbie Weil. Debbie's author of the corporate blogging book and runs a blog of her own on
[00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:13.040]   corporate blogging. Related articles by Xamanta, WebStrategy, the many challenges of writing a
[00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:18.480]   CEO blog. This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI, with permission from Dave.
