[00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:14.880]   Quill-pronounced Cool, launched to great hype about ex-Googlers taking on their former employer
[00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:21.440]   with a 121,617-892-2092-page index (that's $121 billion if you're not good at counting digits),
[00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:23.760]   supposedly making Quill the world's biggest search engine.
[00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.560]   Excerpt from their info page, rather than rely on superficial popularity metrics,
[00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.840]   Quill searches for and ranks pages based on their content and relevance.
[00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:36.720]   When we find a page with your keywords, we stay on that page and analyze the rest of its content,
[00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.720]   its concepts, their interrelationships, and the page's coherency, page rank.
[00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:45.360]   A superficial popularity metric? There's a clear roundhouse thrown at Google if I've ever seen one.
[00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.120]   I'm sorry, but didn't page rank crush keyword frequency in internet search because the latter
