[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from July 31, 2008 titled Alfresco,
[00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:09.760]   A+ in positioning as the SharePoint alternative. Frequent readers will know on a pretty tough
[00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.000]   grader, but I have to give Alfresco an A+ for the positioning and strategy around if not the
[00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.560]   naming of today's launch of Alfresco Labs Beta 3. They're drowning in coverage. Press this link
[00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.280]   to see a list. And the positioning and strategy is simply superb. Why? By positioning as the
[00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.120]   Microsoft SharePoint alternative, they get to dismiss the entire existing Enterprise Content
[00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.000]   Management, ECMels category, including their most direct and threatening competitors, for example,
[00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:39.360]   EMC/Documentum, Open Text Interwoven, EMC/Documentum, Open Text Interwoven. The SharePoint threat to
[00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.400]   the existing category is real enough, and the existing vendors wounded, confused, or over-engineered
[00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.120]   enough to make that dismissal credible. Alfresco then gets to have an elevator pitch that boils
[00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:54.320]   down to, everyone knows SharePoint is going to eat the ECM category, and most people like neither
[00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.480]   SharePoint nor Microsoft, so wouldn't you like to have an alternative? It's beautiful in it.
[00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:03.200]   Simplicity, logic, and credible dismissal of what I'd guess is their top short-term enemy.
[00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:07.440]   Most vendors try to dismiss the current competition in their pitches, but it's not credible.
[00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.960]   T-Point Bursal. Hey, either say "We have no competition," yawn, or "We welcome competition
[00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.280]   from the 87-foot giant because it's going to validate our space," in which you may likely
[00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:22.080]   end up roadkill. I'm not sure I've ever seen a startup so elegantly, effectively, and credibly
[00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:27.040]   dismiss a $1 billion plus competitor. What's better is that the strategy backs the messaging.
[00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:31.200]   By effectively offering an alternative SharePoint backend, they are able to swap out the plumbing
[00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.600]   and eliminate the need for underlying Microsoft infrastructure such as SQL Server and Windows
[00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.880]   itself. Great strategy, great messaging, great execution. Well done, John and Ian.
[00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:45.680]   Related articles by Xamanta. Alfresco wants to stand in for SharePoint Server. Introducing
[00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:51.360]   Alfresco Lab3. Alfresco creates SharePoint clone Alfresco as a SharePoint alternative.
