[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from August 2, 2008 titled "Thoughts on
[00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.680]   Category Creation and Information Access Platforms Revised." Revised August Techet,
[00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.200]   2008, still working on cleaning up this consciousness stream.
[00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.760]   Back in the old days, it seemed easy to create a category in software. Look at the database
[00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:25.520]   market, for example. IBM invents the relational DBMSRDB/MS category, Oracle, Ingress, and Informix
[00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.640]   enter in a largely undifferentiated way. Though Informix eventually drifts towards the low-end/
[00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:36.560]   cheap segment, Sybase creates the derivative category of high-performance OLTP RDBMS.
[00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:41.120]   Arbor rechristens the failed multidimensional DBMS as the OLAP server.
[00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.880]   Tandem creates the nonstop RDBMS with its superb fault tolerance.
