[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.440]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from August 22, 2008, titled "The Specialized Database Argument – Performance."
[00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:16.000]   People sometimes ask, "What's the argument for special-purpose databases like MarkLogic, as opposed to general-purpose databases like DB2, Oracle, or SQL Server?"
[00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.600]   While I have written much on this topic, in the end I think it boils down to one word – performance.
[00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:28.900]   The big three database oligopoly have proven that the general-purpose database management system, DBMS, can indeed be bloated into a wide scope of functionality.
[00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:37.240]   Today's RDBMSs are so bloated that most analysts now drop the R, because they've long since stopped being relational.
[00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:42.900]   So while the big three can bloat the DBMS, what they can't do is optimize it for each special case.
[00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:47.800]   By definition, the general-purpose DBMS needs to be optimized for general purposes.
[00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.040]   When trade-offs are encountered, you must design for the general case.
[00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.840]   That's what creates the opening for specialized DBMSs.
[00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:10.440]   For example, MarkLogic is not optimized for the general case – a bit of transaction processing, a bit of data warehousing, a bit of analytics, a bit of text, a bit of XML, a bit of spatial indexing, a bit of data mining, a bit of huge deployments, a bit of tiny ones, a bit of OLAP, a bit of memory residency, and so on.
[00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:17.540]   MarkLogic is optimized for the specific case of large amounts of semi-structured XML data, typically containing lots of text.
[00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:22.000]   The result – performance numbers that simply crush the competition when they're playing in our house.
[00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.400]   For example, while I can't go into specifics, one of our technical staff sent an email out this morning that went like, "Is this magic?"
[00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:34.540]   No. While I think the world of our engineering team and I do believe they have built a tremendous product, there's no magic.
[00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:42.140]   It's simply the combination of a great implementation focused on a specific XML-based use case. No general-purpose player can beat that.
[00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:46.200]   This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI, with permission from Dave.
