[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from September 23, 2008 titled Venture Capital,
[00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.600]   quaint by comparison. I've had several people ask me what it's like to run a venture-backed
[00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.840]   startup with the current mess on Wall Street. My short answer, from a financing point of view,
[00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.080]   is that not much has changed. You can see why if you look at how the VC business works,
[00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:23.120]   venture firms run venture funds. Venture funds are typically tenure, illiquid partnerships.
[00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:27.920]   A fund typically has one general partner, GP, and multiple limited partners, ELPs.
[00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:31.280]   The ELPs are the investors who commit capital at the start of the fund.
[00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.680]   ELPs give that capital to the fund over the first few years through a series of capital calls.
[00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:40.160]   ELPs who miss capital calls are typically diluted out of the fund through draconian
[00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.520]   provisions in the partnership agreement. The GP runs the fund and earns nice fees for so doing,
[00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:50.640]   typically 2 to 3 percent/year of the committed capital and plus 20 to 30 percent of the upside.
[00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.880]   The fixed fees pay for healthy base salaries and the tasteful but not extravagant offices
[00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.600]   on Sand Hill Road. The upside cut is what buys Gulf Streams and Ranches in Montana.
[00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.400]   The GP follows some process to decide which early-stage companies it wants to invest in.
[00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:09.040]   The fund buys preferred stock of those companies, founders and employees typically hold common,
[00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.840]   the fund holds those shares, seeking an eventual liquidity event, such as an IPO or acquisition.
[00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:18.960]   Next to credit default swaps, complex derivatives, and 30,001 leverage ratios,
[00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.040]   the venture capital business looks almost quaint by comparison, using no leverage,
[00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.320]   buy and hold the stock of a portfolio of early-stage companies.
[00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.400]   Aside, speaking of leverage, I thought I'd share this quote du jour that I found on Infectious
[00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.320]   Greed. What is truly disgraceful is that investment banks could only manage returns
[00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.960]   on equity of 15 to 25 percent with a balance sheet that was often leveraged to the sky.
[00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:44.000]   Nils Jensen and Jan-Will Helmsen, of Absolute Return Partners with a 30,001 leverage ratio,
[00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.400]   it means the underlying investments are returning.
[00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.400]   This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI, with permission from Dave.
