[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.780]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from October 1, 2008 titled 'To SaaS'
[00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.240]   or 'Not to SaaS', that is the question.
[00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.600]   Revised, rewritten, and replacing a post from yesterday.
[00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.180]   One question we encounter with our information and media customers is whether they should
[00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.880]   buy MarkLogic Server and build an application on top of it, or use a SaaS offering which
[00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:24.280]   may or may not be based on MarkLogic and effectively rent the use of an application to meet their
[00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.300]   online publishing needs.
[00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:30.120]   The primary arguments in favour of the 'Rent to SaaS' approach are, you get up and running
[00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.440]   faster because you're renting the use of an existing application.
[00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.040]   You have lower upfront fees because you need neither to build your application nor buy
[00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.800]   the hardware/software platform on which to run it.
[00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.180]   You can focus on what matters because you are liberated from the nitty-gritty of building
[00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.180]   and deploying production systems.
[00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.160]   The primary arguments in favour of the build approach are, you create a unique offering
[00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.640]   which you can use to differentiate from your competition, your costs are potentially lower
