[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.680]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from November 13, 2008 titled "Unlearning the
[00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.840]   Relational Model." Thanks to a Google Alert, I stumbled into this interesting post entitled
[00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.080]   "The Content Imperative, Unlearning the Relational Model in Another CEO Blog,"
[00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:19.200]   that of Jola Musseau of Montreal-based IFAsoft. Says Joel, "Relational data content is created
[00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.280]   to be human-readable content can be rendered in multiple presentation formats such as print,
[00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.520]   web, and wireless devices. Therefore, it is very important to claim a separate content from
[00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:32.640]   presentation. Content can have an inherent deep hierarchical structure. For example,
[00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:38.800]   think about the book/part/chapter/section/subsection/paragraph hierarchy.
[00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.800]   The relationships between content items are expressed through hierarchical containment and
[00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:49.040]   hyperlinks. Content is often mixed in the sense of mixed content in XML. For example, inside a
[00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:54.320]   paragraph, some words are italicized, in bold, or underlined, to indicate special meaning.
[00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.080]   Content can have multi-valued properties such as the authors of a document.
[00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.040]   Multi-valued properties are not supported by SQL. He continues, starting an argument
[00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:08.720]   in favor of XML, RDBMS Web20A, and goes on to discuss XQuery.
[00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:12.400]   The relational data model is based on set theory and predicate logic.
[00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.560]   Data is represented as n-ary relations and manipulated with relational algebra.
[00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:22.160]   CMS vendors and even standard bodies have tried to fork SQL in order to support hierarchies
[00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:27.120]   and multi-value properties. It is clear, however, that XQuery is a superior alternative,
[00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.760]   specifically designed to address those content-related concerns, and then finally argues in
[00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:37.040]   favor of XML databases over a JCR repository when dealing with large amounts of content.
[00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.320]   I couldn't agree more.
[00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:42.960]   Hey, I think I like this guy. The post also includes some discussion of data versus content
[00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:48.560]   modeling and some interesting parallel history between SGML/XML and the RDBMS.
[00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.720]   This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI, with permission from Dave.
