[00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.560]   A Must Read." Every once in a while I sneak the odd book review into my blog, and this time,
[00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.040]   the selection is Freakonomics, a rogue economist explores the hidden side of everything by Stephen
[00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.680]   D. Levitt and Stephen J. Duber. This is not exactly a timely review. The book was published
[00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:26.080]   in 2005 and, as of 2007, it had sold over 3 million copies. As it turns out, Freakonomics
[00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.240]   is the first book that I've read cover to cover to cover, so to speak, on my Kindle.
[00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.480]   While I've purchased a few Kindle books, for example, A Useless Tax Guide, a David Baldaccio
[00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:39.040]   novel, and Against the Gods, and several Kindle French newspapers, you can score what's usually
[00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:44.160]   tomorrow's issue of Le Monde for joint $75, for whatever reason I'd never before managed to get
[00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.880]   all the way through a work on my Kindle. It could be the device, it could be the books. Time and
[00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.520]   experience will tell. Levitt describes himself as a rogue economist, but I think of him more as a
[00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.000]   rogue data junkie or data miner. The guy loves to pore through data and look for interesting and
[00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.800]   often non-obvious correlations, sometimes over very long periods of time. Among other issues,
[00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.600]   the book investigates questions such as "What is the effect of information asymmetry between a
[00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.480]   client and a realtor?" "How can you discover cheating through mining large data volumes,
[00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:17.440]   either in schools or among sumo wrestlers?" "Why do crack cocaine dealers tolerate such
[00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.480]   low pay and poor, that is, often deadly working conditions?" "What explains the relatively
[00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:27.040]   sharp drop-off in violent crime in the 1990s?" "What percent of the drop is explained by more
[00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:32.400]   police?" "What explains the rest?" And "Boy, will you find the answer controversial." "Does
[00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.520]   good parenting matter?" "Do mommy and me and me and other Head Start programs actually increase
[00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.880]   test score?" S later in life? What about habits like daily oral reading? What is the sociological
[00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.360]   impact of a child's name? One of the bittersweet tales surrounding Levitt is that he and his wife
[00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:51.360]   lost a baby to meningitis several years ago, prompting him to join a support group for
[00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.600]   parents who'd lost children. During his involvement with that group, he was shocked at the number of
[00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.080]   children who drowned in the family pool, prompting him to start up his data mining engine, eventually
[00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:05.200]   arriving at the following conclusion, "If you have both a gun and a swimming pool, your child is 100
