[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from December 3rd, 2008, titled "Two
[00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.600]   Peoples Separated by a Common Language." I'm in the UK this week, ostensibly for London online,
[00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.680]   but have been so busy that I've yet to make it to the show. Digging through email this morning,
[00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.680]   I took a brief break, headed over to Facebook to check for personal messages, and was greeted by
[00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.640]   the following hilarious dialogue box. It brought to mind the old George Bernard Shaw quote about
[00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.920]   the English and the Americans, "Two people separated by a common language." A few technology-related
[00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:32.480]   points. While I applaud Facebook's efforts at translation, they clearly should not be trying
[00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:38.240]   to translate content from US to UK English, however tempting that may be. Language recognition
[00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.560]   software is readily available, so they could easily have used it to determine that my profile
[00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:47.120]   and status updates are all in English. If they were really clever, looking at the locations of
[00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.960]   my friends and the language of their status updates, they could tell that I speak both French
[00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.440]   and English. And if they simply used metadata, they could have accessed the languages I speak
[00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:02.240]   Facebook application and see that I speak. English, French, and Latin, six years. Sometimes the answer
[00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:07.120]   is indeed hiding in plain sight. This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI,
