[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from December 10th, 2008 titled Rise Early,
[00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.440]   Work Hard, and Strike Oil. I was reading Worth Magazine the other day and stumbled into a one
[00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.240]   page interview with Gordon Getty, son of oil tycoon John Paul Getty. Perhaps I'm the last
[00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.280]   guy on the planet to hear this quote, but I thought it was too good not to share.
[00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.520]   Increasingly, if you read books like Fool by Randomness, you begin to believe that the luck
[00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.920]   part of the equation is indeed essential. That is, luck compounded by the increasing
[00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.320]   returns of leadership complemented by competence may indeed produce far superior results to good,
[00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:35.280]   old-fashioned hard work. If that sounds obtuse, read my review of Fool by Randomness here.
[00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.640]   As an example of the concept, let's examine one of my favorite industries,
[00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:45.040]   venture capital. Two otherwise identical VC firms, A and B, are founded in year X. Both produce
[00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:50.480]   identical IRRs on their first 29 investments. On investment 30, firm A has a giant hit,
[00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:56.480]   producing an overall IRR of 39% as opposed to B's 25%. So, in every respect but one,
[00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.760]   the firms are identical. And let's say the difference resulted from blind luck.
[00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.200]   Firm B missed the giant hit not because they passed on the investment opportunity,
[00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.000]   but because of a random, non-business related event. What happens next? Firm A is seen as more
[00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.160]   skilled than firm B in the market by investors, entrepreneurs, entrepreneurs, entrepreneurs on
[00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:17.280]   other venture capitalists. Firm A can raise money more easily than firm B. Firm A can attract higher
[00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.920]   quality new partners than firm B. Firm A can often invest – ceteris paribus – at lower
[00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.040]   valuations than firm B from A, therefore yields – ceteris paribus – a higher IR than firm B.
[00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:31.600]   In essence, a self-fulfilling prophecy results, catalyzed by a random event.
[00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.720]   Now, I'm not saying that the VC business is pure luck. To pass the ceteris peribustus,
[00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.920]   with top VCs, you must assemble a team of very smart people to pick investments and sit on boards.
[00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.520]   And VCs provide very different levels of marketing support to their companies.
[00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:49.840]   A strong point of Sequoia, by the way. And VCs provide very different network benefits
[00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:54.400]   in terms of introductions to suppliers, customers, partners, and potential acquirers.
[00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:59.520]   I believe those benefits are real and do explain some of the variance in outcomes amongst VC firms.
[00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.080]   I am saying, however, that the VC business is most certainly not pure skill either.
[00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.520]   I believe that both luck and increasing returns most certainly play a part,
[00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:12.640]   the extent of which neither I nor, unfortunately for most investors, anyone else actually knows.
[00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.960]   To wrap up, let's turn back to Jean-Paul Getty. Half a century before Fooled by Randomness was
[00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.440]   written, I think he captured the idea nicely – rise early, work hard, and strike oil.
[00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.040]   Which, on an optimistic note, brings to mind the famous Samuel Goldwyn quote,
[00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:28.960]   "The harder I work, the luckier I get." Related articles by Zamanta.
