[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from December 29, 2008, titled "Marketers Beware,
[00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.800]   Even Dilbert Says Everything is a Platform." Thanks to a post by Darren Cunningham on Lucidaire's
[00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.440]   blog, I found this wonderful Dilbert cartoon, which concludes that "everything is a platform."
[00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:19.920]   I was happy to find this because tech marketers have so thoroughly overused the word "platform"
[00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.200]   that I now rarely use it myself, even when I think it would have been appropriate.
[00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:28.800]   More thoughts? Using platform to avoid product. "Product" is not a four-letter word,
[00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.320]   I'm amazed at the number of companies that try to use any word other than "product"
[00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:38.800]   to describe their offering. Example? Well, our platform does XYZ, using solution to avoid product.
[00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.440]   I'm similarly amazed at the number of companies who do a global find/replace on the word "product"
[00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.400]   with the word "solution" in their marketing. Example? Our drill bit solutions do XYZ.
[00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:55.120]   Just say our products do XYZ. Just say our products do XYZ or more simply, our drill bits do XYZ.
[00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.120]   The intent of solutions marketing is not to replace the word "product" with "the."
[00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.880]   Word solution? It's to lead your sales and marketing by talking about problems that
[00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:07.920]   customers worry about solving instead of your product's features. Example? Talk about vendor
[00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.800]   managed inventory to retailers, a problem instead of aggregate awareness, a BI tool performance
[00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:18.320]   feature. Use of platform to sound strategic. One of my pet peeves if when people use platform
[00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:22.800]   as a synonym for product because it sounds more strategic, in reality a platform is something you
[00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.600]   build upon, so if you don't have some sort of API then you can't be a platform. I remember in the
[00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.360]   early days of business objects, most people considered "tool," a four-letter word, which
[00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:35.680]   technically it is, but you get the idea. People wanted to say anything but "tool" because tools
[00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:40.240]   were cheap and tools were not strategic. Over time, BI did in fact become a platform but only
[00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:44.720]   after APIs were added and then fixed and slowly people started building applications on top.
[00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:48.640]   Similarly, in its early days Salesforce was most certainly not a platform but an application
[00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.880]   delivered as a service. Today, however, that's different because they offer force on a platform
[00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.840]   as a service, pay-as-basis, and people build on it. Net/net, if no one's building on top,
[00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:02.320]   it's not a platform. So marketers, figure out what your offering is and then be proud to call
[00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.720]   it that. Don't call it a platform when it's not a platform because you think it sounds strategic.
[00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.520]   You'll only confuse yourself and your customers in turn.
[00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.520]   This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI with permission from Dave.
