[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.980]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from December 31, 2008, titled "2008,
[00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:08.540]   The Year Internet Surpassed Newspapers."
[00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:12.580]   See this net article by Dan Farber entitled "Print News is Fading," but the content
[00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:13.580]   lives on.
[00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:17.380]   Among other things, he presents the result of a recent Pew Research survey that showed
[00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.680]   the internet passing newspapers in 2008 in response to the question "Where do you get
[00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:24.340]   most of your national and international news?"
[00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:28.560]   From Farber's article, NBC's Jeff Zucker said recently, "I love the quote about trading
[00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.280]   'dollars' for dimes."
[00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.520]   I think it captures the problem perfectly.
[00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.520]   Much as the 2000-era quote "selling dollars for 90 cents" captured the essence of most
[00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.040]   internet business models.
[00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.840]   I believe we are still very much in transition between internet and print models because
[00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:45.920]   the internet crowd is getting a free ride from the major news organizations.
[00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.600]   I'm not sure how it's going to work out in the end, but I've learned that innovators'
[00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.480]   dilemma-style transitions can take decades to play out.
[00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.360]   First, you need to damage the disrupted badly enough to force them to change, which can take
[00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:57.360]   years.
[00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.520]   Again, you need the disrupted to either go out of business or change before you reach
[00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:04.680]   steady state, because there can be a long free ride period where the disrupted is enabling
[00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:05.880]   the disruptors.
[00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:10.280]   For example, what would Google News be if there were no New York Times, AP, CNN, and
[00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:11.280]   AFP?
[00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.760]   You can only give away something that exists and someone is somehow going to have to pay,
[00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:18.880]   either directly or indirectly, for the creation of free content.
[00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.340]   Only when newspapers stop bleeding and start dying in numbers will we be able to see how
