[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from March 25, 2009, titled, "If at first you
[00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.480]   don't succeed, should you try, try again." Check out this article in the New York Times that
[00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:15.360]   overturned Silicon Valley conventional wisdom about failure. Per a Harvard Business School
[00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:21.440]   working paper, which looked at several thousand venture capital-backed companies from 1986 to 2003,
[00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.320]   first-time entrepreneurs had a 22% chance of success. Already successful entrepreneurs had
[00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:33.120]   a 34% chance of success, a 55% relative increase. Previously failed entrepreneurs had a 23% chance
[00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.000]   of success. That is, the lessons from having tried and failed added up to a 1% overall increase in
[00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.480]   the success rate, surprising news for a valley in which failure is often seen as a red badge
[00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.920]   of courage. Exurbed, "The data are absolutely clear," says Paul A. Gompers, a professor of
[00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.320]   business administration at the school and one of the study's authors. "Does failure breed new
[00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.960]   knowledge or experience that can be leveraged into performance the second time around?" he asks.
[00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:02.160]   In some cases, yes, but overall, he says, "We found there is no benefit in terms of performance."
[00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:07.840]   The New York Times article is here. The complete working paper is here, PDF 35 pages.
