[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.840]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from April 2nd, 2009 titled "Amazon Elastic
[00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:10.880]   MapReduce – Power to Burn on Demand." Amazon Web Services today announced Amazon
[00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.600]   Elastic MapReduce, a new member of the Amazon Web Services family designed to help users process
[00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.520]   vast amounts of data using the divide-and-conquer parallel processing approach made famous by
[00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.480]   Google's MapReduce and as implemented in the Apache Hadoop project. Background on Hadoop from
[00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:30.080]   the project site. Scalable, Hadoop can reliably store and process petabytes. Economical, it
[00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.480]   distributes the data and processing across clusters of commonly available computers.
[00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.880]   These clusters can number into the thousands of nodes. Efficient by distributing the data,
[00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.440]   Hadoop can process it in parallel on the nodes where the data is located. This makes it extremely
[00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.640]   rapid. Reliable, Hadoop automatically maintains multiple copies of data and automatically redeploys
[00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.960]   computing tasks based on failures. Here's some background on MapReduce from Google Labs,
[00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:57.760]   from Google Labs. So Amazon Elastic MapReduce is a cloud-based service that enables you to
[00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:03.280]   perform highly parallel operations against large amounts of data, all in an on-demand model.
[00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:07.520]   This strikes me as a great offering, particularly for those organizations who have an intermittent
[00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:13.040]   need for large Hadoop clusters. From the Amazon press release, it utilizes a hosted Hadoop
[00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:17.840]   framework running on the web-scale infrastructure of Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud, Amazon EC2,
[00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:23.120]   and Amazon Symbol Storage Service, Amazon S3. Using Amazon Elastic MapReduce, you can instantly
[00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.760]   provision as much or as little capacity as you like to perform data-intensive tasks for distributed
[00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:32.800]   applications such as web indexing, data mining, log file analysis, machine learning, financial
[00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:38.960]   analysis, scientific simulation, and bioinformatics research. As with all AWS services, Amazon Elastic
[00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:42.960]   MapReduce customers will still only pay for what they use, with no upfront pain,
[00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.920]   ends, or commitments. Amazon says they made the offering in response to users who were already
[00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:52.800]   deploying Hadoop clusters on their lower-level EC2 framework. That is, that this was an organic
[00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.480]   evolution. Some researchers and developers already run Hadoop on Amazon EC2, and many of them have
[00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:03.760]   asked for even simpler tools for large-scale data analysis, said Adam Salipski, Vice President of
[00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.800]   Product Management and Developer Relations for Amazon Web Services. Amazon Elastic MapReduce
[00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.600]   makes crunching in the cloud much easier as it dramatically reduces the time, effort, complexity,
[00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.160]   and cost of performing data-intensive tasks. I suspect this was a bad day at Cloudera,
[00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.040]   an Excel-backed startup that wants to be the red hat of Hadoop. Perhaps, like SugarCRM and competing
[00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.280]   against Salesforce, Cloudera will soon offer an on-demand Hadoop as well. But that means
[00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.680]   supporting two business models at once and buying a lot of hardware to boot. And, I suspect, a lot
[00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:36.480]   more hardware than SugarCRM needs to buy to support sales automation as a service. This audio version
