[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.700]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from April 8, 2009 titled "Regulating
[00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:06.700]   Venture Capital?
[00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:07.860]   Me thinks not."
[00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.800]   What if you went to the doctor's office with a sore wrist and she proposed bandaging
[00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:11.800]   your ankle?
[00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:15.000]   That's how I feel about the government's proposal that venture capital be regulated
[00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.020]   along with other private capital pools, including hedge and private equity funds.
[00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:23.480]   See this Mercury news story, "Venture Capital Needs Transparency Not Regulation" for background.
[00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:27.660]   I'm no financial expert, but far as I can tell, the root causes of our current financial
[00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.920]   crisis are leverage.
[00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:34.520]   Investment banks and hedge funds building 30,001 levered portfolios and somehow managing
[00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:38.940]   to only get 8-10% returns on them kind of reminds you of buying on margin as a root
[00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.660]   cause for the crash of 1929.
[00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.680]   Financial system interlocking and the too big to fail problem.
[00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:49.520]   Like it or not, as a citizen and taxpayer, it does seem to me that many of these firms/funds
[00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.760]   are indeed too big to fail and the government was correct to use my/our money to stop the
[00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:54.840]   collapse.
[00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.720]   Agency problems and excess compensation.
[00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:00.680]   Basically, you had very smart people who could make 10 million dollars plus per year by taking
[00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:01.920]   excess risk.
[00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.480]   When viewed from their perspective, put undiplomatically, who cares what happens to their employers?
[00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.240]   It doesn't take many years for example 1 of 10 million dollars' income to become
[00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:11.240]   PE.
[00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.040]   Urminantly wealthy so senior managers had huge agency issues where the interests of the owner
[00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.920]   and the agent diverge, which seemingly were left unchecked both by the company's own
[00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.400]   boards and by government regulators.
[00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.960]   The housing bubble and the conflicts of interests among loan originating banks, assessors, developers,
[00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:27.400]   and mortgage brokers.
[00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:32.800]   Arguably, the root cause here is the securitization of mortgages combined with the next point.
[00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.280]   Conflicts of interest in the rating system.
[00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:39.000]   I never knew this before, but the people who pay ratings agencies are the issuers of debt,
[00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.240]   not the buyers.
[00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:44.440]   This would be like Sony paying consumer reports to rate their new television sets.
[00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:48.520]   Perhaps this is how a portfolio of zero-down, floating rate mortgages on overpriced houses
[00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.960]   in Stockton gets rated AAA.
[00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.920]   The let's ensure each other problem associated with credit default swaps.
[00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:58.680]   In a tightly interlinked system where each player is too big to fail, this strikes me
[00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:03.160]   as a mathematical hallucination designed to make it look like each player is taking L.
[00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:04.260]   SA risk.
[00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.240]   But in reality, it seems like a bunch of people living on the same street in Florida ensuring
[00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.120]   each other against hurricanes.
[00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:14.400]   The question isn't when will the insurance system fail, but indeed will it ever work.
[00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.440]   That is, will there ever be a hurricane that wipes out only a few of the houses in the
[00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:18.440]   pool?
[00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:22.720]   Lack of regulation to control/keep and check the amount of risk, leverage, ratings, and
[00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:23.720]   agency issues.
[00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:27.360]   I'm sure I missed some, and if you think I got anything wrong in this laundry list,
[00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.120]   feel free to comment, because my primary point is that nowhere on this long list will you
[00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.440]   find anything related to venture capital.
[00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.800]   In fact, as I've previously argued, venture capital looks quaint by comparison.
[00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:42.800]   VCs buy and hold the shares of startup companies on 5-year, plus or minus timeframes.
[00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:43.800]   No leverage.
[00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:45.560]   No ratings agencies.
[00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:49.520]   Investment professionals, for example, foundation managers are typically the only investors,
[00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:51.680]   so there's no duping of John Q. Public.
[00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.480]   Yes, venture returns are down over the past decade.
[00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.080]   Yes, there are probably too many VC firms, and a shakeout is imminent.
[00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.200]   Yes, VCs make lots of bad investments.
[00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:04.880]   Yes, VC is increasingly a hits business, where the biggest winners in the portfolio account
[00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.840]   for a disproportionate share of the returns.
[00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.800]   Yes, VCs can make a lot of money.
[00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.400]   And yes, I view carried interest as income and not capital gains.
[00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:18.320]   And yes, there is probably an element of fool-by-randomness/increasing returns inherent in the VC pecking order.
[00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.880]   Sure, there are lots of flaws, but overall I believe the VC system works.
[00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:27.040]   Much as you might say democracy is the least bad form of government, VC strikes me as the
[00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:30.200]   least bad way of driving innovation in the economy.
[00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.600]   It wasn't part of the problem, so let's leave it out as part of the solution.
[00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.200]   I know the ratings problem is more subtle and involves mixing loans of various quality
[00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.440]   to stay just, within the bounds of a given creditworthiness level.
[00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:44.440]   Nevertheless, I'd argue a good rating system would differentiate between a basket of all
[00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:48.860]   solid loans and a basket which mixes solid, semi-solid, and wobbly ones.
[00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:52.220]   As an aside, and largely from a position of pure ignorance, I'm amazed that someone
[00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:55.560]   hasn't raised some venture capital and tried to challenge the ratings industry with a new
[00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:00.080]   consumer capital and tried to challenge the ratings industry with a new consumer-focused model.
