[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.840]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from April 17, 2009 titled "A Crack in the IPO
[00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.600]   Window - Rosetta Stone IPO." In the third IPO in April, language education provider Rosetta Stone
[00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:18.000]   went public this week, raising $112.5 million. The IPO priced at $18, and the stock ended its
[00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.800]   first day of trading at $25.12, a 40% rise. As one banker said to me, "This shows that investors are
[00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.800]   getting back into the business of investing." I think that's a good thing, not only because
[00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.000]   I run a private venture back company, but more importantly, because a closed IPO window
[00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.480]   one locks out John Q. Publitt from buying the shares of early. And mid-stage companies,
[00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.560]   two forces some companies to be sold before their time, potentially snubbing the lives of
[00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:46.960]   would-be, great independent companies, and three indirectly reduces the attractiveness
[00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.840]   of the venture capital machine that I've long argued is a highly effective engine for driving I.
[00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.760]   Novation in the Economy
[00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.200]   Numbers-wise, Rosetta Stone is quite a bit above what I'd been calling the
[00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.880]   50/50/E.I.P.O. window that I've seen in the software equity group's IPO pipeline data,
[00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:09.680]   $50 million plus in TTN revenues, 50% plus growth, E.R. percent plus E.B., I.T.D.R.,
[00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:16.640]   Rosetta Stone's key numbers are 2008 revenue, $209 million, 2008 growth, 52% adjusted E.B.I.,
