[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from June 11, 2009 titled "Critical Thinkers
[00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.240]   vs. Critics." One of the most important skills underlying a successful business career is,
[00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:15.520]   in my estimation, the ability to think critically. While I think it's fairly obvious why critical
[00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:20.080]   thinking is important, that is, better decision-making, I'm often surprised by how poorly
[00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:25.520]   many, many executives do it. I make a distinction between critical thinkers and critics. I don't
[00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.080]   subscribe to the management maxim. Never raise a problem unless you have a proposed solution,
[00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:35.120]   for several reasons. Some big, hairy problems don't have obvious solutions and thus may never
[00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.080]   get discussed. The maxim encourages the submission of contrived solutions in order to avoid getting
[00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:44.960]   blasted for not proposing one. Some people may be in a perfect position to spot problems but have
[00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.440]   no idea how to fix them. For example, the valet at a restaurant may overhear numerous
[00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.360]   complaints about the vichasoise but isn't about to tell the chef how to fix it.
[00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.600]   But the intent of the maxim isn't all wrong. Nobody wants to work with a whiner who complains
[00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.560]   all day about the organization's problems. I make a distinction between one critic — a person who
[00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:08.080]   criticizes everything, generally without proposed solutions — and two critical thinker — a person
[00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.240]   who attacks ideas in the spirit of making them better and who can hold both sides of an argument
[00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.880]   in their head at once. I think I'm a pretty good critical thinker. But, on the other hand, maybe
[00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:22.240]   not. Hint, joke. At one point, I had a boss who loved to stop me in meetings and say, "Dave,
[00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.880]   you're arguing with yourself." I think he viewed that tendency as a weakness that wasted time in
[00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.600]   the meeting. I viewed it as a strength. I sometimes think I should have replied, "Well, I need to
[00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.320]   argue with someone who can hold their own against me, boss." Yes, that's another joke. Here's a
[00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:40.800]   quick, one-question test to help you determine if you're a critical thinker or a critic. Do you
[00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.520]   attack your own ideas as well as those of others? Critics attack other people's ideas but not their
[00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:50.800]   own. Critical thinkers attach everyone's ideas, especially their own. For certain disciplines,
[00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.520]   for example, marketing positioning. One of my primary tests is not to examine the substance
[00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.320]   of a proposal, but instead to examine the critical thinking in the process that led to it. How many
[00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:05.040]   other taglines did you think of? Why didn't you pick tagline number three? Did you consider taglines
[00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.760]   based on the higher-level notion of satisfaction? What's the argument against the tagline you're
[00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:13.200]   proposing? What are the direct and indirect competitor's taglines and their relative
[00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:18.240]   strengths and weaknesses? As David Ogilvy once said, "Good writing is slavery. See page 33 of
[00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:23.440]   Ogilvy on advertising. So is good positioning. And it comes from critical thinking and plenty of it."
[00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.360]   Here's another test to see if you're a critical thinker. Can you articulate the other side of your
[00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.160]   argument so well that folks who support it would hire you to do so? If not, then you've not really
[00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.240]   understood it. You've not really thought critically. You've looked at the flip side superficially and
[00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.720]   dismissed it. Here's a third test which may sound a bit obsessive. Do you constantly worry that you're
[00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.200]   not critical thinking? That you're somehow trapped so in the box that you can't see there is a box?
[00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.280]   If so, I think that's a very good sign. The other thing that sometimes impedes critical
[00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:54.400]   thinking is experience. Many times I've seen executives just hit rewind/play on business
[00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:00.480]   ideas. Well, this worked at Oracle, SAP, Cadence, IBME, so it's going to work here. But will it?
[00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.680]   Are we actually in the same situation as Oracle, SAP, Cadence, or IBM was when the idea worked?
[00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.560]   It's amazing how many people fall into that trap. The other big experience trap is false knowledge.
[00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.800]   Example, I know that great salespeople always have something to prove. How do you know that great
[00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:19.360]   salespeople always have something to prove? Well, indignantly, I've been at this 20 years and it's
[00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.160]   true of every great salesperson I've ever seen. Response, did every great salesperson you've
[00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:28.480]   worked with also have a belly button? This reminds me of Jim Collins' observation and good-to-great
[00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.400]   that all great companies have buildings. So do all bad ones. You should be looking for what
[00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.640]   differentiates good salespeople/companies from bad ones, not just for commonalities among the
[00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:42.560]   good ones. Well, Mr. Bob, the greatest sales VP I've ever seen, said it was true. As the old saw goes,
[00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.800]   sales VP I've ever seen said it was true. It ain't what you don't know that will hurt you. It's what
[00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.160]   you know that ain't so. The fact is most successful people have no idea why they have been successful.
[00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:56.960]   They may have risen early, they may have shook 100 hands a day, they may carry to rabbit's foot,
[00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:00.640]   and they may have been quite successful. But for each billionaire early riser,
[00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:05.840]   I can find scores of early risers/100 hand shakers/rabbit's foot carriers who aren't.
[00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:09.200]   What's the solution to avoid these experience traps? Critical thinking.
[00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:14.560]   By the way, how do I know that all of above is true? I don't. I think it is. There is some
[00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:19.360]   science behind a few of the views, for example, Russo's books and similar ones. But in the end,
[00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:25.280]   it's just my opinion. This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI with permission from Dave.
