[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from June 13, 2009 titled "Things Not To Do,
[00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.120]   Declare Your Category Dead." I was reading this interesting post,
[00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:13.120]   "Emeriting Enterprise Content Management Trends," on the Gilbane Group blog this morning when I
[00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:17.760]   stumbled into this rather amazing soundbite. Jeff Fried, VP Product Management for Microsoft's
[00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.640]   Fast Search Engine, actually proclaimed that "keyword search is dead." Now, last I checked,
[00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.680]   Fast was doing around $50 million. Oh, sorry, I mean post-correction of accounting irregularities,
[00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.080]   $35 million a quarter in enterprise search revenue, and that Microsoft paid $1 billion
[00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.720]   for the company in order to do a "best defense is a good offense" strategy versus Google.
[00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.400]   So regardless of what Brother Fried or his PR mavens think, I can assure you that Microsoft
[00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:44.720]   doesn't think keyword search is dead. Oh, and did I forget to mention, as they say in Brooklyn,
[00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:48.800]   "bada bing?" One of my pet peeves is people or companies who think it's cool or controversial
[00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.840]   to declare themselves dead. Why? The first time I heard something was dead was in 1987 at the
[00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.400]   original ingress. The thing in question was the relational database. At one company meeting,
[00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:03.040]   our executives patiently explained to us unwashed employees that because of the ANSI SQL standard
[00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:08.400]   relational databases were commoditizing and ergo that we would be de-investing in the core RDBMS
[00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.240]   engine and instead investing in application development tools. I'm not sure there's a
[00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:19.280]   font big enough to write this, but oh, oh, PS. In 1987, the RDBMS market in total was maybe $200
[00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:26.400]   million. Today, it's approximately $10 billion oligopoly shared by Oracle, IBM, and Microsoft.
[00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.680]   Application development tools are somewhere between 1/10th and 1/100th, the size and the
[00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.560]   high fragmented market by comparison. What went wrong? Lack of understanding of product
[00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.440]   differentiation. Yes, the products were arguably becoming more similar due to the SQL standard,
[00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:47.360]   for example. Ingress still primarily spoke well at the time, but more similar, identical, identical,
[00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.720]   commoditized. The possibilities of high-speed, low-cost, parallel-optimized, query-optimized,
[00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:57.280]   platform-optimized, non-stop, or a dozen other possible bases of differentiation seem to elude
[00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:01.200]   ingress management. My take if people can differentiate white rice for example,
[00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.400]   regular, par-boiled, in a bag, basmati, jasmine, texmati, then you can sure as hell differentiate
[00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:12.960]   techno. Ogi. Non-observance of industry structure in RDBMS. Product differences is just one piece of
[00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:18.720]   degree of rivalry, in Michael Porter's 5 forces analysis. Substitute threats were low, that is,
[00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.840]   switching costs were high, buyer power was low, barriers to entry were high, and supplier power
[00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:28.720]   was low. By seeing only product and not seeing industry structure, Ingress missed that a huge
[00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:33.520]   oligopolistic market was in formation. Only Oracle seemed to really get that a land grab was in
[00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:37.920]   progress, that switching costs were high, and that the goal should be to get as much market share as
[00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.520]   possible in the short-term, even at the cost of making a mess, which you could then sort out later,
[00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.560]   missing industry structure and application development tools. The flip side of the attractive
[00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.520]   industry structure in RDBMS was a rather appalling industry structure and application development
[00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.880]   tools. Barriers to entry were low, competitors were numerous, the large number of competitors
[00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:03.280]   was putting downward pressure on prices, and the dawn of free runtimes was already underway.
[00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.000]   Simply put, it's very hard to make money in tools and that's one reason why tool
[00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:13.280]   really is a four-letter word on Sandhill Road. Confusing being up the stack with value. One
[00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.600]   argument is that RDBMS is just plumbing and that tools are higher in the stack and/or go
[00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:23.200]   deliver more value and more potential for profit. This is wrong. Why? Because while tools are indeed
[00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.760]   higher up the stack, profit potential comes from industry structure, not stack altitude.
[00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.480]   Microsoft makes plenty of profit and they are at the bottom of the stack. The Oracle DBMS business
[00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:38.000]   in one level up and is a key driver of Oracle's 40% operating margins. There are many misconceptions
[00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:43.200]   about the application's business in this regard, but I woah. Not go there now. See the profit zone
[00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:48.320]   for more on this general topic. Too much emphasis on vision. If your vision for the future goes out
[00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:52.160]   so far that we're all dead, then perhaps you should dial it back a bit to make it useful.
[00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:57.840]   Yes, we're all dead in a hundred years and one day RDBMS services may be as commoditized as
[00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:03.680]   electricity, but some 20 years later, RDBMS's are nowhere near a commodity and a lot of people have
[00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.920]   made a lot of money in the meantime. It's not predicting the eventual end, that's the hard part.
[00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.360]   It's figuring out what happens along the way and how to make money during that evolution.
[00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:15.680]   So before you go ahead and declare your business dead, ask yourself some questions. Am I doing
[00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:20.080]   this for PR Soundbyte? If so, is it really the kind of message you want to communicate?
[00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:23.760]   Is this the best you can do to sound visionary? If you really believe it, have you critically
[00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.360]   thought your view or are you being mentally lazy with the "in the long run we're all dead and
[00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:31.600]   everything's a commodity" argument? If you really believe it, then should you turn in your badge and
[00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.160]   let someone run the business who doesn't? This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI
