[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from June 16, 2009, titled Log Me In
[00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:10.640]   Files Again for IPO. Another tech company, Log Me In, lit up the blogosphere, for example,
[00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:16.960]   this story today by pricing its IPO at $114 to $16 for 6.67 million shares.
[00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.760]   It's been a long road to hoe for Log Me In, they filed their original S1 in January 2008.
[00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:27.520]   The revised S1 was filed June 16, 2009. Here are some quick highlights,
[00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:36.160]   2008 revenues of $51.7 million, 2008 revenue growth of 91% 2008 operating income of $5.4 million,
[00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:44.560]   down from $9.2 million in 2007, 1 Q09 revenue of $7.2 million, 1 Q09 year-over-year revenue
[00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:53.120]   growth of 72%, 1 Q09 operating income of $3.7 million, 2008 sales and marketing costs of 61%
[00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:58.800]   of revenue, $27 million in cash, pre-financing. At first blush, you might wonder how the sales
[00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.560]   and marketing intensive money-losing operation can be generating cash and the answer is,
[00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:08.400]   alas, a high-growth subscription business. You sell annual subscriptions, 94% have a one-year term,
[00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.800]   take the cash upfront, and amortize the revenue over the year. This explains part of it.
[00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:17.280]   But the rest comes in a renewal rate, which they say is currently 80%, page 61.
[00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:24.080]   Let's do some quick, very rough math. If 80% of the 2007 revenue renews, then of the $51.7 million
[00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:31.600]   in 2008 revenue, $30.1 million is new and $21.6 million is recurring. Since the sales and marketing,
[00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.800]   S&M cost of renewals should be negligible, you should more properly analyze S&M expense as a
[00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:42.000]   percent of new revenues, not total revenues. When you do so, you see that S&M as a percent of new
[00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.640]   revenues is 105%, so they're spending $1.05 to get each $1.00 in new revenue. Does that make
