[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from July 2nd, 2009 titled "Stonebreaker,
[00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.800]   Send Relational DBMSs to the Home for Tired Software."
[00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:13.680]   Mike Stonebreaker spoke today at SIGMODC Tweetstream where, among other things,
[00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.720]   there was a 40-year anniversary celebration of the relational DBMS and, in what I suspect
[00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.320]   is non-coincidental timing, Mike did a post on the CACM site entitled "The End of a DBMS
[00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.840]   Era Might Be Upon Us." Excerpt, his key argument is all about performance, in any given use case.
[00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.160]   Stonebreaker thinks RDBMSs can be beaten by about a factor of 50.
[00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:40.400]   In data warehousing, he says a column store wins by 50 times. In OLTP, he says a memory resident,
[00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:46.720]   DBMSs wins by 50 times. For scientific data, he says a DBMS specialized for the job can win
[00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:52.400]   by 50 times. For RDF, he says, column stores do a reasonable job and is confident that specialized
[00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:57.520]   RDF triple stores will do better, that is, 50 times or more. I'd add that at MarkLogic,
[00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:01.920]   we think we do a reasonable job as well. For text, he points out that no major search engine
[00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.720]   uses a relational database so they didn't even qualify for consideration. For XML,
[00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.880]   he cites a private report I sent him a while. Backdone for one of our customers comparing
[00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:16.800]   MarkLogic performance to a relational DBMS. When on our turf, we usually win by no less than 10
[00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:22.880]   times and sometimes 100 times or more. Sometimes, queries are not even processable in an RDBMS
[00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:27.680]   and/or need to be hand-optimized and hand-joined between a DBMS and a search engine.
[00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:32.960]   He reduces to three cases how special-purpose DBMS vendors get their advantage. A non-relational
[00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:37.600]   data model. A different implementation of tables. A different implementation of transactions were in
[00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:42.640]   the first category, using XML as our data model instead of a table. It's a great post. Check
[00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.320]   it out and check out the cited references as well. This audio version was created by
