[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.680]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from August 9, 2009, titled "NY Times
[00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.520]   Calls Out Tom Siebel on Death of IT Claims."
[00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.360]   About six months ago, I blogged my notes from a speech given to the Alliance of Chief Executives
[00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:18.800]   by Tom Siebel, entitled "From IT to ET, From Information Technology to Energy Technology."
[00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.220]   While I enjoyed the speech, I had a few problems with it.
[00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:26.000]   It struck me as unduly and anecdotally negative on the future of information technology.
[00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.580]   It reeked of a "the party's over because I'm leaving" mentality.
[00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.420]   Some of it felt like pure spin.
[00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:36.160]   When talking about his vuVenture, C3, which I think stands for carbon-conscious consumer,
[00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.280]   Siebel seemed to go miles out of his way to position it as an energy technology company,
[00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.680]   not an information technology company.
[00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:46.100]   But as far as I could tell, it was going to make a software application to help enterprises
[00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.320]   manage their carbon footprint, kind of an ERP for carbon.
[00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:55.360]   Quoting Siebel, "an application to monitor, monetize, and mitigate the carbon footprint."
[00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:57.480]   To me, that's clearly information technology.
[00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.160]   Basically, it felt like his thesis was "IT is dead."
[00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:06.880]   L. On live ET and any facts that contradicted it were overlooked or spun away.
[00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.880]   So I was happy to see this piece in the New York Times this morning are the glory days
[00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:13.560]   long gone for IT, which challenges some of Siebel's claims.
[00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:17.520]   The chart above shows the perils of defining the world by one's own experience.
[00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:23.600]   While Siebel characterized 1980 to 2000, Siebel's prime years, as the go-go years of IT, in
[00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.720]   reality, the fastest period of IT growth was between 1961 and 1971.
[00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.360]   Timothy Bresnahan, a Stanford economist, similarly does not accept Mr. Siebel's contention
[00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.480]   that the decline in growth rates this decade, which encompasses two recessions, signals
[00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:41.040]   a permanent end to IT's record of growing faster than the larger economy.
[00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.160]   "It is early days to say the game is over," he said.
[00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.360]   To me, the latter point is critical.
[00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:50.160]   Rather than defining the future of the industry by its recent growth rate or by one person's
[00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.120]   successes, we should define it in terms of work left to do.
[00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.560]   And from that perspective, there is plenty of work left and plenty of growth associated
[00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:57.560]   with that work.
[00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:02.480]   In my estimation, it's not that 1980 to 2000 were the two big decades of IT, they were
[00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.040]   the two decades of data.
[00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:08.080]   The growth that Siebel refers to was all driven by the relational database, the applications
