[00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:55.840]   Our second point is that NoSQL is part of a broader trend in database systems specialization.
[00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:03.760]   The jack-of-all-trades relational database, for example, Oracle, DB2, works reasonably well for a broad range of applications, but it is a master of none.
[00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:10.400]   For any specific application, you can design a specialized DBMS that will outperform Oracle by 10-1,000 times.
[00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:16.200]   Specialization represents IN, aggregate, the biggest threat to the big 3DBMS oligopolists.
[00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.200]   Examples of specialized DBMSs include...
[00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:27.200]   StreamBase, Skyler, real-time stream processing, MarkLogic, semi-structured data, Vertica, Greenplum, mid-range data warehousing,
[00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:34.960]   ASTER, large-scale AKA big data, analytic data warehousing, VoltDB, high-volume transaction processing, MATLAB, scientific data management.
[00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.880]   Our third point is that NoSQL is largely orthogonal to specialization.
[00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:46.560]   There are specialized NoSQL databases, for example, MarkLogic, and there are specialized SQL databases, for example, ASTER, Volt.
[00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.280]   The only case where I think there are zero examples is general purpose, NoSQL systems.
[00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:57.280]   While I'm sure many of the NoSQL systems, QL crowd, would argue that their systems can do everything.
