[00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:57.040]   If you take offense at the word "proprietary", that's fine. You can call them unique instead.
[00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:04.240]   But the point is an application written on Cassandra is not practically moved to Couch, regardless of whether you can access the source code both Couch and Cassandra.
[00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:11.760]   Our sixth point is that we think MarkLogic provides the best of both worlds option between open source NoSQL systems and traditional DBMSs.
[00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:22.000]   Like open source NoSQL systems, MarkLogic provides shared-nothing clustering on inexpensive hardware, superior support for unstructured data, document orientation, and high performance.
