[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.520]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from February 16, 2011, titled "Strategic
[00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.440]   Thoughts on Finding a Job in Silicon Valley." While I know that reading the newspaper, with
[00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.680]   high unemployment, record budget deficits, and drastic spending cuts, might make you want to
[00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.440]   go back to bed in the morning, from my experience there is plenty of positive excitement happening
[00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:22.960]   in Silicon Valley right now. Venture capital, VC, is the engine of Silicon Valley, VC investment
[00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.680]   is strong, and entrepreneurship seems to be alive and well. After finishing up a six-year run at my
[00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.240]   last company, I am currently in the process of looking for my next opportunity. Since I've been
[00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:36.320]   out and about and thinking quite a bit about the job search process, I thought I'd share a few of
[00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:42.240]   my learnings along the way. Opportunity trumps execution. Seek companies that face large and/or
[00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:46.880]   obvious market opportunities. I have always done best when joining companies where I am convinced
[00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:51.840]   that everybody needs one. Team trumps position. Being on the right team is more important than
[00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:57.040]   the particular position you're asked to play. Positions change over time. Don't pick a director
[00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:01.200]   title at a weaker company when you could have made five times the money, had five times the fun,
[00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.040]   and made five times more valuable networking relationships as a senior product manager at a
[00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:10.960]   stronger one. Business card narcissism can be a road TT. Oh, nowhere. See the bottom of this
[00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:17.760]   post for some fun math in this regard. Think IPO zone, not pre or post. IPO. Most people draw a
[00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.800]   bright line at a company's IPO, acting as if the good part of the movie ends there. In reality,
[00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:28.160]   an IPO is like high school graduation. It is the beginning, not the end. If you can join a quality
[00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:33.600]   company in the IPO zone, for example, 12 months either side of an IPO, you are likely to do very
[00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.280]   well. Which side of the IPO line matters far less than whether company is reasonably in the IPO zone.
[00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:44.480]   Thanks to Jerry Held for helping me reframe things this W. I know thyself. Get a sense
[00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:48.320]   for what kind of environment you will realistically like and then use interviews to validate or
[00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:53.600]   invalidate that view. For example, I've talked to companies ranging from 3 to 300,000 employees,
[00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.720]   and I can say that I most enjoy the 100 to 10,000 range. Yes, that's two orders of magnitude,
[00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.360]   but I've talked across five. Have a positioning. I have worked hard to keep my positioning strategic
[00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.320]   marketing guy. You might think I would have dumped marketing guy in favor of CEO during the past six
[00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:11.920]   years, but I deliberately did not for two reasons I thought it would not have environment you will
[00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:22.320]   realistic and dowsing range. In the end, nobody grows up a CEO. We all grow up in some function
[00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:27.200]   that helps define who we are. Yes, I have been a successful CEO, think I'm process-oriented,
[00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.200]   and think I'm great at running and scaling operations, but deep down, I'm an analytical,
[00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.320]   strategic marketing guy for New York. It's essence versus experience. Positioning is about essence.
[00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:40.560]   Remember that like sales, it's a volume game. I have looked at over 40 different opportunities
[00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.280]   in one month and keep finding new ones every day. I've donned either through networking with peers
[00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:49.520]   and venture capitalists, cultivating recruiter relationships over the years, and to a lesser
[00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:53.920]   extent, by leveraging social media. At some point, you will pick a new role, and you will make a more
[00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:58.640]   informed choice if you have really beaten the bushes while searching. Be picky. Life's too short,
[00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:03.280]   and we spend too much time at work to work with people we don't genuinely enjoy. For me, that means
[00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.760]   finding smart, direct people who are just a little bit crazy. For you, it probably means something
[00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.480]   else. But all of us should people idly avoid bossholes, or boardholes, or learning capitalists,
[00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:18.480]   or our boardholes. Who are. You win things for everyone. Be nice. A CEO friend has a board
[00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.800]   member who is fond of saying, "Friends come and go, enemies accumulate." That's great advice
[00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.120]   to remember both in day-to-day work life as well as when you're out looking for a new opportunity.
[00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.120]   It's a small valley, and you want to accumulate as few enemies as possible during your time working
[00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:35.520]   in it. Bonus, some fun employment math first. Let me make a table that demonstrates two rules
[00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:40.720]   of thumb. Salary increases 30% with each level in an organization, and equity increases 30%
[00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:45.520]   with each level in an organization, and equity increases four times. Summation of a B-round
[00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:52.560]   startup below. Note that, while the CEO makes 4.6 times a clerk's salary, he or she makes 1 to 24
[00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.360]   times a clerk's equity. That is the argument for being high in an organization. But we have to be
[00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:02.400]   careful not to apply that logic blindly. Let's take an example. Say you're good enough to get a VP
[00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.760]   job at a good quality startup. That might come with 1% equity grant. Now, let's say you're talking to
[00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.640]   another, better quality startup and they want to make you a director with a 0.3% equity grant,
[00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.960]   but because you're a hot candidate you can talk them up to 0.5%. Let's say the
[00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:22.400]   stronger company is growing 80% and the weaker one 30%. You make 3.5 times as much money with
[00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:26.640]   the smaller grant, and smaller title, at the stronger startup. Note that even if you can't
[00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:31.680]   talk up the grant to 0.5% and only get the 0.3% initially offered, you still make over
[00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:35.360]   twice the money at the stronger company. Which company will look better on your resume in the
[00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:40.320]   future? And if you're good, who's to say you won't end up a VP at the stronger company in year 2?
[00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.600]   Hopefully this demonstrates how company opportunity trumps job title,
[00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:47.120]   i.e. that being on the right team is more important than the position you're initially
[00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:52.160]   asked to play. This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI with permission from Dave.
