[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from March 12, 2011, titled "An Amazing
[00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.800]   Story About Twitter and the Japan Earthquake". Every once in a while I have an aha moment
[00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:13.840]   where I am blown away by an unsuspected use or combination of technologies.
[00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.440]   Prior to yesterday, the last such moment was when I heard my son shouting in French while
[00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.560]   playing alone in his room on a new game console.
[00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:33.280]   Had he gone crazy, I thought? Then it clicked. I knew the console was internet-connected,
[00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.920]   I knew it had a Bluetooth headset, I knew it supported multiplayer games, and I knew
[00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:40.120]   he spoke French. It had just never occurred to me that it would all come together such
[00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.360]   that he'd end up playing video games with kids in France and talking to them while so
[00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:44.360]   doing.
[00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.280]   Yesterday, I had a similar moment while I was talking to a friend with family in Japan.
[00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:51.800]   We discussed the recent earthquake and she told me the following story. While the earthquake
[00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:55.960]   was still in progress, this blew me away. Think about that. Someone can tweet about
[00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:59.720]   an earthquake as it hits. You can get the tweet 5000 miles away and call your friend
[00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:03.440]   while the earthquake is still happening. In fact, once I really started to think about
[00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.440]   it, I realized that you can actually call your friend before the earthquake arrives
