[00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.320]   But you are trained to approach business with humility and with an experimental spirit.
[00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.180]   "Me, but what if it isn't?
[00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:15.180]   What if we're wrong?
[00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:16.700]   Heck, it's not a bad idea.
[00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.880]   But we dreamed it up in two hours on a white board.
[00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.680]   The problem is that things fail all the time in business.
[00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:22.680]   Products fail.
[00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:23.680]   Startups fail.
[00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:24.680]   Business models fail.
[00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.000]   Heck, Sundance failed.
[00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.760]   And the bigger problem is that when we dismiss the possibility of failure in our planning,
[00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:32.600]   we dismiss the possibility of learning along with it."
[00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:38.640]   Yes, business, and particularly so in startup upland, is the quest for finding a repeatable,
