[00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.160]   in eight different cities selling to numerous types of different target customers with a
[00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.800]   variety of different sales pitches.
[00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.520]   Consider these scenarios.
[00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:13.520]   Everybody sells.
[00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:14.680]   Buy more stock quick.
[00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:17.160]   Anyone can sell this stuff to anybody saying pretty much anything.
[00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.580]   Hint, this does not happen very often.
[00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:20.880]   Some sell and some don't.
[00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:21.880]   This is tricky.
[00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:26.040]   Did the folks who sold sell because of their background, their territory, the target customer,
[00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.640]   their approach, or their sales pitch?
[00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:29.000]   Well, we don't know.
[00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.560]   We can look for patterns, but we haven't designed the experiment to make things easy.
[00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.560]   The quick assumption is that the folks who sold did everything right and the folks who
[00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:38.960]   didn't did everything right and the folks who didn't did everything wrong.
[00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.600]   But if you think about it, you can't assume that's the K.
[00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:45.720]   C) Did we have a great salesperson in Chicago pitching the wrong message?
[00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.640]   The guy in DC who sold a lot carries a rabbit's foot.
[00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.900]   Did we dispatch rabbit's feet instantly to the whole sales force?
[00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.720]   After firing the VP of Sales, we learned that world class actually meant "I liked him/her"
[00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:00.080]   and can find virtually no additional common traits among the sales force.
[00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:01.920]   Hum, nobody sells.
[00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:03.040]   This is hard too.
[00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:04.920]   Was everybody doing everything wrong?
[00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:05.920]   Unlikely.
[00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:08.720]   Yet no one came together with the right combination to sell.
[00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:11.480]   But are we sure the lady in Chicago is a bad salesperson?
[00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:15.120]   Can we be sure that the pitch they're using in DC doesn't work?
[00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:20.160]   Can we be certain that there is no market for the product as the VP of Sales is insisting?
[00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.480]   What can we learn from such a random experiment?
[00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:23.680]   The answer is nothing.
[00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.200]   Thus, my statement, if we can't have repeatable success then can we at least have repeatable
[00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:29.320]   failure?
[00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:34.200]   If instead of hiring eight salespeople we hired only three, put them all in NYC and called
[00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.000]   only on the same handful of titles within investment banks using the same sales presentation
[00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.600]   and demo, could we have learned more?
[00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:41.600]   Yes.
[00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.200]   If it works, it's great news, because you know exactly what to go scale.
[00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.280]   If it doesn't work, it's still good news, perhaps for your successor, because you can,
[00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:54.240]   with a pretty high degree of certainty, conclude that mix of levers tried will not work.
[00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.080]   Or in the spirit of Thomas Edison, you've learned one more way not to make a light bulb.
[00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.240]   I've picked two extreme cases and there certainly is middle ground in between, but
[00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:06.660]   the two key questions are, are we assuming only successful outcomes in our planning?
[00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:10.800]   Are we designing things as an experiment from which we can learn no matter the outcome?
