[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from May 10th, 2011 titled "The Open Source
[00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:11.120]   Software Paradox." As a marketer, I'm a fan of open source software. After all, if you can't
[00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:16.320]   dislodge Microsoft from midrange server operating systems, Microsoft Office from desktop productivity
[00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.440]   suites, or Oracle from relational databases, and doing so through traditional means is a
[00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.240]   virtual impossibility. Then blowing up the whole business model isn't a bad start.
[00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.360]   It's creative, and it cuts right to the core of the problem. But as a businessperson, I am not.
[00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.200]   When you play the role of market spoiler, it's much easier to be famous than rich.
[00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:41.680]   For example, when MySQL was acquired by Sun in 2008 for 1.2 billion dollars, MySQL was doing
[00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:46.720]   only about 65 million dollars in annual revenues. While the revenue multiple on the exit was
[00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:52.960]   spectacular, their capture rate was not. MySQL disrupted literally billions in Big3, that is,
[00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.960]   Oracle, DB2, SQL Server database revenues. But if your value proposition is rooted in
[00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.280]   almost free relative to leading commercial alternatives, then you won't succeed at 50%
[00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:09.840]   if they. Our cost? You'll need to be more like 2-5%. I refer to open source as both a development
[00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.560]   model, that is, a way of building software, and a business model. While the former is more well
[00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.800]   defined than the latter, the typical way to make money in open source is through selling subscriptions
[00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.600]   or licenses to certified and more quickly patched releases as well as selling technical support and
[00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.640]   or consulting services to go with them. While a spectacular exit multiple may occasionally
[00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:34.720]   pay off big time for shareholders, for example, JBoss, MySQL, my theory is that in general,
[00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:39.840]   it's very hard to make money with the open source business model. Red Hat is the obvious exception,
[00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.240]   and we'll talk about them in a minute. The basic paradox of open source is this.
[00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.920]   The smaller the community, the worse the software quality, and the more people need
[00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:53.200]   certified releases and support. The bigger the community, the higher quality the software,
[00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:58.880]   and the less people need certified releases and support, that is, the community version will do.
[00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:03.360]   So you can have a large community who doesn't need to buy from you, or a small community who does.
[00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.320]   Two other drivers complete the picture. The nature of the software, and to what extent it
[00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:13.280]   truly requires an almost daily stream of patches and updates and the monetization rate which is a
[00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:18.000]   function of the commercial market structure. For example, the lower level the software, for example,
[00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.000]   operating systems, the more the market tends towards natural monopoly as customers want
[00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.400]   to minimize entropy at the bottom of the stack. This should drive high pricing/margons on the
[00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.080]   commercial side of the market and a parallel opportunity for someone to establish clear
[00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.320]   leadership on the open source side. This is why Red Hat does establish clear leadership on the
[00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:39.440]   open source side, and most others end up stagnating in the tens of millions of revenues range.
[00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.720]   The market is huge, the software is low level and thus the market wants, a clear leader think,
[00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:49.600]   increasing returns, who can provide a hardware-independent, low-cost supported product as an
[00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.640]   alternative to the proprietary Unix ease of days past. Put differently, the bigger the commercial
[00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.440]   market and the more monopolistic its structure, the better the open source opportunity. Conversely,
[00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:03.680]   the smaller the commercial market and the more fragmented leadership is within it. For example,
[00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:09.040]   enterprise search, document management, and to some extent BI, the worse the open source opportunity.
[00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:13.200]   This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI with permission from Dave.
