[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from May 19, 2011, titled "A Fun Taxonomy
[00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.800]   of Technology Executives." Building on… on a post by TenGen's Max Shiresen, which in turn
[00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.520]   built on a post by me, I thought I'd have some fun by playing with and enhancing Max's taxonomy
[00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:20.400]   of technology senior executives. Max's theory is that a surprisingly number of executives have just
[00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.040]   one play in their business playbooks which fall into a number of categories. Building on Max's
[00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.240]   grouping and, based on my 20+ years in business, here is mine, the bandleader. They get the old
[00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.720]   band back together from a prior company. Bandleaders are often surprisingly hands-off
[00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.040]   managers who swear by their teams and travel with them from gig to gig. They often alienate
[00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.720]   existing employees, viewing themselves as professionals compared to the regime they are
[00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:47.280]   replacing. These types are effective to the extent that the band's capabilities are aligned with the
[00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.560]   company's needs. Joe Process. Joe's never met a problem that can't be solved with Process.
[00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:57.600]   Consultants, methodologies, training, flowcharts, and stoplight, based performance dashboards appear
[00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:03.600]   from. The Woodwork. Joe was effective to the extent that lack of Process is a company's problem.
[00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.160]   In Joe's world, by the way, that includes everything. Even a strategy problem is a process
[00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.040]   problem. We just need a good strategy process. What Joe fails to grasp is that knowing how to
[00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.400]   do things is different from knowing what to do. The Strategist. Strategists focus on developing
[00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.720]   a deep understanding of the company's current situation and then evaluating future scenarios
[00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:27.600]   based on it. Good strategists are quantitative as well as qualitative in their analysis,
[00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.160]   paying attention not only to business and M, marketing strategies but also the resources
[00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.640]   required to execute them. Bad strategists forget what I call the strategy compiler,
[00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.200]   that is, for a given company in a given situation with a given set of resources and capabilities,
[00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:46.640]   is a chosen strategy executable. A great strategy that's only executable by some other company is
