[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from July 26, 2011 titled "Bobby Fisher
[00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.880]   Applied to Silicon Valley – Pattern Matching vs. Good Moves."
[00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:13.440]   When asked why he won so many matches, chess grandmaster Bobby Fischer would reply, "All
[00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.040]   that matters on the chessboard is good moves.
[00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:21.480]   That is, winning is all about the moves, and moves, in turn, are all about the situation."
[00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.240]   Contrast this to today's Silicon Valley fashion of pattern matching which seems the
[00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:29.120]   opposite – all about the players and not so much about the moves.
[00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.280]   Consider Blippi, a bad idea if there ever was one, which created a $13 million BC sinkhole
[00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:37.120]   for a service to share credit card receipts on your social network.
[00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.000]   Let's look at the founders, two recent Stanford engineering grads, and an experienced entrepreneur,
[00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.560]   Philip Kaplan, most famous for bubble era website, Fekit Company.
[00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:46.560]   How about Quile?
[00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.200]   Pronounced Kewail, Quile launched in July 2008 claiming to be the next Google with superior
[00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.960]   indexing and operational cost advantages.
[00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:57.800]   It seemed clear to me – and the world – that from the start, Quile wasn't any better
[00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:58.880]   than Google.
[00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:03.440]   They burned $33 million in VC and entered the TechCrunch Deadpool in Sept 2010.
[00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.760]   Let's look at the founders, three ex-Google engineers, two of them PhDs and one from Stanford.
[00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:12.880]   When pattern matching is the rage, when the moves are so obviously bad, and when the players
[00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:17.280]   so clearly match the pattern, I'd argue that Blippi and Quile broke Fischer's law.
[00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.800]   They weren't about the moves, they were about the players.
[00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.080]   I used to joke that if you wanted to raise money in Silicon Valley you should be aware
[00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:25.800]   that VCs see people in one of four buckets.
[00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:26.920]   Made me money before.
[00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.000]   Made someone money before.
[00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.000]   Went to Stanford.
[00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.000]   Everybody else now.
[00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.000]   Make no mistake.
[00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.640]   The team has always been a key factor in venture capital investment.
[00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:37.120]   But I think the historical approach was to see the team as de-risking element for the
[00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:38.120]   idea.
[00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:41.080]   Put differently, we are investing in a market opportunity and we would like to isolate as
[00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.300]   much risk as possible to the market opportunity.
[00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:44.300]   How do we do that?
[00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.120]   By getting an experienced executive team to reduce execution risk?
[00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.720]   By hiring experienced engineers to reduce product development risk, etc.
[00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:54.120]   That hiring experienced engineers to reduce product development risk, etc.
[00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:55.120]   That's enfertly.
[00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.880]   We are investing in a market opportunity.
[00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.220]   Valentine used to say, "Great markets make great companies."
[00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:04.920]   Asides, irony alert in the above video where Don tells a bunch of Stanford graduate students,
[00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.000]   it doesn't matter where they go to school and note further that Valentine was a pithy
[00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.040]   quote machine, coming up with such classics as, "I am 100% behind my CEOs up until the
[00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.120]   minute I fire them, and all companies that go out of business do so for the same reason
[00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:18.120]   they run out of money."
[00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:22.100]   Somehow, I wonder if things haven't gotten upside down of late, where the players matter
[00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.120]   more than the moves.
[00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.280]   I'd argue that Silicon Valley used to be about the moves, the strategy and market opportunity,
[00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:31.400]   and VC sought experienced players as a risk reduction technique.
[00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:35.260]   Now, it appears to be about the players and the implicit assumption that those who match
[00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:38.760]   the player pattern can win any match, regardless of the moves.
