[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from March 2, 2012 titled "The Right Time
[00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.200]   to Raise Money at a Startup." I'm often asked by entrepreneurs, "When is the right time to
[00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.320]   raise money at a startup?" I invariably say two things in response, whenever you can. Right now,
[00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:19.040]   whenever you can. Most startups typically go through ups and downs. Say you've just completed
[00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.680]   four consecutive quarters above plan. Your growth is high and your burn rate is reasonable. You have
[00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.840]   enough cash to go three more quarters before you need money. What should you do? Unless you are
[00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.800]   virtually certain that your quarterly streak will continue, I'd say raise money. Why? Because the
[00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.160]   increase in valuation/decrease in dilution from adding one to two more quarters to the streak
[00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.800]   is nothing compared to the decrease in valuation/increase in dilution from tanking a quarter along
[00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.040]   the way. Whether you're a subscription or perpetual company, investors will always want to
[00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:52.000]   see new sales/new bookings as your primary growth metric. While the SAS model does damp revenue
[00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.200]   volatility, that's precisely why VC will want to see bookings. And, in my experience, bookings are
[00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.960]   volatile. During my 24 quarters at MarkLogic, we hit our bookings targets about 90p. Arcent of the
[00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:08.080]   time. But I can say, sometimes, when we missed, we missed. I remember one quarter coming in around
[00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.200]   50% of target. Right after that quarter is exactly when you do not want to be raising money, and, by
[00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.680]   the way, it's usually precisely when you need it. The fastest way to end up bridge loans, where you
[00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:22.000]   lose almost all control of your company and are fed milestone to milestone, is to not raise money
[00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.880]   when skies are blue and instead try to raise money in a storm. Much as I love them, VCs are not in
[00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.120]   the business to be nice people. If you're coming to them hat in hand with 30 days of cash in the
[00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.200]   bank, I can assure you that you will not raise money on favorable terms. You could have done
[00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.080]   that 90 days ago, but if you've tanked the quarter, now it's too late. To show this in reverse, one
[00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.720]   trick VCs love is to sneak a peek at an extra quarter. I remember one time when I was raising
[00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:48.400]   money, we'd agreed on terms, and the lawyers said it should take two to three weeks to close the
[00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.480]   round. It was June 12th, so if everybody pushed hard, we should have been able to close the round
[00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:57.920]   before the quarter ended on June 30th. But suddenly everyone disappeared. Hello? Hello?
[00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.840]   Why aren't the VCs calling back? How come their lawyers have gone silent and are taking forever
[00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:07.920]   to turn paper? Hello? Hello? We made the quarter and the round closed July 5th. Arguably, I could
[00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.000]   have gone back and asked for a higher valuation based on having made the quarter, that is, knowing
[00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.480]   that 2Q would be successful wasn't priced into the round. But the VCs are good, they knew I
[00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.960]   wouldn't do that and they wouldn't have let me if I tried. So they got a free peek at the 2Q results.
[00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:24.320]   I'll bet you $1,000 that if we tanked that quarter, they'd have come back to me seeking
[00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:29.280]   to lower the valuation. The game is neither fair nor symmetric. So time your round to close before
[00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.600]   the fair nor symmetric. So time your round to close before the first day of the last month of
[00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.920]   the quarter right now. Particularly for new companies, I believe the right time to raise
[00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.080]   VC money is right now. Too many would-be entrepreneurs treat fundraising like going
[00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:46.080]   to the senior prom. I need to find a date, I need to book a limo, I need to do my hair,
[00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.480]   I need to get a dress. Translation, I couldn't possibly go talk to VCs about raising money
[00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:55.440]   until I have a great flight deck, an advisory board, a CEO, a beta product, or some customers.
[00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:00.240]   To me, it's all avoidance. Traditional A-round VCs want to catch you in your dorm room.
[00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:04.480]   They are used to talking PhD students or dropouts about their visionary ideas.
[00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:08.560]   They are not worried about whether you have a CXO. In fact, they would be more than happy
[00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.960]   to help you find the CXO which, by the way, I wouldn't recommend. They are worried primarily
[00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:17.680]   about the market opportunity, your idea, and your technology. As Don Valentine always said,
[00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:21.760]   "Great markets make great companies." What's more, they want to invest in people they know.
[00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.240]   One way to get known is to build a relationship over time as you think about raising money for
[00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.720]   your company. The best way to do this is to find someone in your network who can connect you to a
