[00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:03.760]   with him." This way you remove the prom factor from your VC meetings because you're not going
[00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:08.400]   on the big one-shot date. You're not raising money. You are thinking of raising money. So it's
[00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:13.120]   not awkward for them to not invest. In fact, they never even need to say no. But if they like your
[00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:16.880]   idea and your company, they'll be shoving money in your pockets whether you ask for it or not.
[00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:20.800]   Better yet, you can invoke their competitive instincts by noting that you're chatting with
[00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:25.360]   several VCs about whether you should raise money. Best of all, this approach lets you benefit from
[00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:29.840]   their wise and free feedback as you develop a relationship over time. If they are even
[00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.080]   moderately interested, they are going to want to track you. That is, "Hey, come back in a
[00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:38.480]   month and let's have a coffee." If you continue to make good progress during that time, that is,
[00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:42.800]   accomplish what you say you will in a given timeframe. You not only build credibility but also
[00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.360]   slowly transform yourself from straw. In good or two, interesting person who I've been tracking
[00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:52.000]   for the past six months. That becomes even more helpful when the VC needs to convince his partners
[00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.200]   to invest in you, as he invariably will. So now you know the secret. When's the right time to raise
[00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.760]   money in a startup? Either right now or whenever you can depending on your situation. No business
[00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.720]   ever died from a little extra delusion. But as the ever-quotable Don Valentine also pointed out,
[00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.240]   all companies that go out of business do so for the same reason they run out of money.
