[00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:33.800]   king.
[00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.880]   Since you're the king, you need to end this war, post-haste.
[00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.320]   The strategic problem with sales and engineering, alignment.
[00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.480]   Sales/engineering tension typically comes from a lack of alignment around strategy.
[00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.620]   Sales, by default, wants to sell anything to anybody and it's up to the CEO to make
[00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:52.320]   sure that sales is ring-fenced enough into a target market that they can't keep generating
[00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.960]   effectively random product requirements and be taken seriously.
[00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:00.080]   Perhaps that end you should create, as an executive team, a one-page document entitled
[00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.400]   Our Target Market that describes, using terse bullets, what the perfect prospect looks like
[00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.280]   when he or she walks through the door.
[00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:09.560]   The more a given customer looks like that ideal, the more their voice should be heard
[00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:11.880]   in the product requirements process.
[00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:16.720]   And conversely, this helps create the notion of strategic versus opportunistic revenue.
[00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:20.680]   The former is revenue coming from the target, the latter is revenue that you will take,
