[00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.520]   style questions that MBA students pondering this example should consider. What is disruption?
[00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.920]   Given Bruce's statement of the case, do you view Siebel as a victim or disruptive innovation or
[00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.160]   a weakening macro environment? Are the effects of disruptive innovation on the disruptee always
[00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.880]   felt directly, or are they indirect? For example, directly might mean losing specific deals as
[00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:10.880]   opposed to indirect where a general stall occurs. What does it feel like to be an executive at a
[00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.360]   disruptee? Do you necessarily know you are being disrupted? How could you separate out what whether
[00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.200]   you are stalling due to the macro environment or a disruptive? How could you separate out what
[00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.800]   whether you are stalling due to the macro environment or a disruptive? You are being
[00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:28.240]   disrupted? Remember the definition of dilemma. Two options and both are bad. While not in the article,
[00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:32.240]   according to friends I have who worked at Siebel, management could be quoted in this time frame as
[00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:37.360]   saying "now is the time to be more Siebel than we've ever been" as opposed to emulating Salesforce.
[00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:41.680]   Comment. What should Siebel have done differently? Was the over-reliance on call center revenue
[00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.080]   making them highly exposed to a downturn in a few verticals? How could they have diversified using
