[00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:37.440]   how to solve." The question, is this a good rule? Let's take an old example from my career.
[00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:43.040]   It's 1990, you work at Ingress, which is $250 million division of Ask, and you compete in the
[00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.600]   relational database market against Oracle, who is about $1 billion. You are getting your
[00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.960]   Ask kicked up and down by Oracle in the RDBMS market. Management is silently executing a
[00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:57.840]   retreat strategy into the application development tools market, and worse yet, your parent company
[00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:03.120]   Ask is betting all in on a new version of Ingress 4GL that only works on the Ingress DBMS for their
[00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:09.120]   next generation ERP system. Here are some darn good problems. Oracle is killing us in the DBMS
[00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.240]   market. We are moving into tools, when run times are increasingly free and there is no money to be
[00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.720]   made. We are double-downing on a proprietary, unstable application development environment
[00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.040]   instead of using standard tools Ask is suffering from a serious escalation of commitment problem
[00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.760]   and should not double-down on a dying database business. If you follow the "don't bring up a problem
[00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:32.240]   without a solution" rule, then you could never talk about any of these problems. And they are
[00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.560]   only the most important problems facing the company, and that would ultimately lead you to its
[00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.680]   undoing. What if, for example, you ran sales and had no idea what application development tools the
[00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.920]   company should be using, but simply knew which it should not? Should you make up a bad solution just
[00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:51.200]   so you can talk about the problem? I can take more recent examples of similar no-easy solution problems.
[00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.800]   What do we do about the internet? At Business Objects in 1996,
[00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:01.520]   when we were 100% Windows client applications, what do we do about NoSQL? At MarkLogic in 2009,
[00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:05.600]   when we were a closed-source non-relational DBMS into a strong open-source trend,
[00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:11.920]   what do we do about Zendesk? At Salesforce in 2012, after acquiring Asset.ly and mistakenly
[00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.480]   seeking synergy versus trying to use it in a major blunting initiative. Let's look beyond a business
[00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.000]   environment and see some problems that we couldn't talk about if we followed the rule.
[00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.200]   Mid-East Peace Cancer Global Warming Sorry, Jimmy, if you don't know
[00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.640]   the solution to global warming, then you shouldn't bring it up because you'll just be whining.
[00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.040]   Obviously, I think it's a stupid rule. The correct rule is don't whine. It turns out the hardest
