[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.800]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from July 12, 2014 titled "Why a CEO I Love Driver."
[00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:10.240]   While driver-based planning is a bit of an old buzzword (the first two Google hits date
[00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:16.000]   to 2009 and 2011 respectively), I am nevertheless a huge fan of driver-based planning not because
[00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.360]   the concept was sexy back in the day, but because it's incredibly useful. In this post, I'll explain
[00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.240]   why. When I talk to finance people, I tend to see two different definitions of driver-based planning.
[00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.800]   Heavy in detail, one where you build a pretty complete bottom-up budget for an organization
[00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.480]   and play around with certain drivers, typically with a strong bias towards what they have
[00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:39.680]   historically been. I would call this driver-based budgeting. Light in detail where you struggle to
[00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:43.360]   find the minimum set of key drivers around which you can pretty accurately model the business,
[00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:47.360]   and where drivers tend to be figures you can benchmark in the industry. I call this driver-based
[00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.080]   modeling. While driver-based budgeting can be an important step in building an operating plan,
[00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.960]   I am actually a bigger fan of drive, R-based modeling. Budgets are very important, no doubt.
[00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.680]   We need them to run plan our business, align our team, hold ourselves accountable for spending,
[00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.800]   drive compensation, and make our targets for the year. Yes, a good CEO cares about that as a sign
[00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:12.080]   qua non, but a great CEO is really all about two things, financial outcomes and how they create
[00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:17.040]   shareholder value, the future, and not just next year but the next view. The ultimate purpose of
[00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.280]   driver-based models is to be able to answer questions like "What happens to key financial
[00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.080]   outcomes like revenue growth, operating margins, and cash flow given set of driver values?"
[00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:30.160]   I believe some CEOs are disappointed with driver-based planning because their finance team have been
[00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.600]   showing them driver-based budgets when they should have been showing them driver-based budgets when
[00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.600]   they should have been showing them driver-based models. The fun part of driver-based modeling
[00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:42.080]   is trying to figure out the minimum set of drivers you need to successfully build a complete P&L for
[00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:47.360]   a business. As a concrete example, I can build a complete, useful model of a SaaS software company
[00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:53.280]   off the following minimum set of drivers. Number and type of sales reps, quota/productivity for
[00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.960]   each type. Hiring plans for each type. Deal bookings mix for each, for example, duration,
[00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:05.600]   prepayments, services. Intro quarter bookings, linearity, services margins. Subscription margins,
[00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:11.520]   sales employee types and ratios, for example, one SE per two sales reps. Marketing as percent of
[00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:17.840]   sales or via a set of funnel conversion assumptions, for example, responses, MQLs, optis, win rate,
[00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:25.200]   ASPR, and D. Aspenn of sales, G and A aspenn of sales. Renewal rate, AR and AP terms. With just
[00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:30.480]   those drivers, I believe I can model almost any SaaS company. In fact, with the more detailed
[00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:36.480]   assumptions, rep types, marketing funnel, I can pretty accurately model most. Finance types
[00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.880]   sometimes forget that the point of driver-based modeling is not to build a budget, so it doesn't
[00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:45.840]   have to be perfect. In fact, the more perfect you make it, the heavier and more complex it gets.
[00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.640]   For example, intro quarter bookings linearity, that is, percent of quarterly bookings by month,
[00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.560]   makes a model more accurate in terms of cash collections and monthly cash balances,
[00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:59.360]   but it also makes it heavier and more complex. Like each link in Marley's chains, each driver
[00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:03.600]   adds to the weight of the model, making it less suited to its ultimate purpose. Thus, with the
[00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.520]   additional of each driver, you need to ask yourself. For the purposes of this model,
[00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:12.400]   does it add value? If not, throw it out. One of the most useful models I ever built assumed
[00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:16.720]   that all orders came in on the last day of quarter. That made building the model much simpler,
[00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.000]   and any sales before the last day of the quarter, of which we hope there are many,
[00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.720]   become upside to the conservative model. Often you don't know in advance how much impact a given
[00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.640]   driver will make. For example, sticking with intro quarter bookings linearity doesn't actually
[00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.240]   change much when you're looking at quarter granularity a few years out. However, if your
[00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:36.160]   company has a low cash balance and you need to model months, then you should probably keep it in.
[00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:40.320]   If not, throw it out. This process makes model building highly iterative.
[00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.520]   Because the quest is not to build the most accurate model but the simplest,
[00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:47.680]   you should start out with a broad set of drivers, build the model, and then play with it.
[00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.200]   If the financial outcomes with which you're concerned and it's always a good idea to check
[00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:55.840]   with the CEO on which these are, you can be surprised or relatively insensitive to a given
[00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:00.160]   driver, throw it out. Finance people often hate this both because they tend to have precision
[00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.800]   DNA which runs counter to simplicity, and because they have to first write and then discard pieces
[00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.880]   of their model which feels wasteful. But if you remember the point, to find the minimum set of
[00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:12.560]   drivers that matter and to build the simplest possible model to show how those key drivers
[00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:17.200]   affect financial outcomes, then you should discard pieces of the model with joy, not regret.
[00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:21.520]   The best driver-based models end up with drivers that are easily benchmarked in the industry.
[00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:26.240]   Thus, the exercise becomes, if we can converge to a value of x on industry benchmark y over
[00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:30.960]   the next three years, what will it do to growth and margins? And then you need to think about how
[00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:35.680]   realistic converging to x is. What about your specific business means you should converge
[00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:40.160]   to a value above or below the benchmark? At Host Analytics, we do a lot of driver-based
[00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:44.880]   modeling and planning internally. I can say it helps me enormously as CEOs think about industry
[00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:50.000]   benchmarks, future scenarios, and how we create value for the shareholders. In fact, all my models
[00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.960]   don't stop at P&L. They go on to implied valuation given growth/profit and ultimately
[00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:59.520]   calculate a range of share prices on the bottom line. The other reason I love driver-based planning
[00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.000]   is more subtle. Much as number theory helps you understand the guts of numbers in mathematics,
[00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.280]   so does driver-based modeling help you understand the guts of your business,
[00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:11.840]   which levers really matter and how much. And that knowledge is invaluable.
[00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:16.720]   This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI with permission from Dave.
