[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.480]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from July 26, 2014, titled "You Can't Analyze
[00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.440]   Churn by Analyzing Churn."
[00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:13.000]   One thing that amazes me is when I hear people talk about how they analyze churn in a cloud,
[00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.140]   software as a service, SaaS, or other recurring revenue business.
[00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:22.760]   You hear things like, "17% of our churn comes from a merging small business ESB segment,"
[00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.840]   which is normal because small businesses are inherently unstable.
[00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:31.120]   22% of our churn comes from companies in the $1 billion-plus revenue range, indicating
[00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.680]   that we may have a problem meeting enterprise needs.
[00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:38.120]   40% of the customers in the residential mortgage business churned, indicating there is something
[00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.660]   wrong or product for that vertical.
[00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.620]   There are three fallacies at work here.
[00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:44.400]   The first is assumed causes.
[00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.200]   If that 17% of your churn comes from the ESB segment, you know one and only one thing.
[00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.200]   That 17% of your churn comes from the ESB segment.
[00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.520]   Asserting small business stability as the cause is pure speculation.
[00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:57.640]   Maybe they did go out of business or get bought.
[00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.260]   Or maybe they didn't like your product.
[00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:02.920]   Or maybe they did like your product but decided it was overkill for their needs.
[00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:06.960]   If you want to know how much of your churn came from a given segment, ask a finance person.
[00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.560]   If you want to know why a customer churned, ask them.
[00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.200]   Companies with relatively small customer bases can do it via a phone.
[00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:16.520]   Customers with bid bases can use an online survey.
[00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:18.000]   It's not hard.
[00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.680]   Use metrics to figure out where your churn comes from.
[00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.560]   Use surveys to figure out why.
[00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.200]   The second is not looking at propensities in the broader customer base.
[00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:31.720]   If I said that 22% of your annual recurring revenue ARR comes from $1 billion plus companies,
[00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:35.640]   then you shouldn't be surprised that 22% of your churn comes from them as well.
[00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.840]   If I said that 50% of your ARR comes from $1 billion plus companies and they were your
[00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.620]   core target market, then you'd be thrilled that only 22% of your churn comes from them.
[00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:47.700]   The point isn't how much of your churn comes from a given segment, it's how much of your
[00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.120]   churn comes from a given segment, it's how much of your churn comes from a given segment
[00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:52.480]   relative to Ho.
[00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.340]   Only much of your overall business comes from that segment.
[00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.720]   Put differently, what is the propensity of someone to churn in one segment versus another?
[00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:03.400]   And you can't perform that analysis without getting a full data set of both customers
[00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.320]   who did churn and customers who didn't.
[00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:09.040]   That's why I say you can't analyze churn by analyzing churn.
[00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.360]   Too many people, when tasked with churn analysis, say, "Quick, get me a list of all the customers
[00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.280]   who churned in the past six months and will look for patterns."
[00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:17.880]   At that instant you are doomed.
[00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:21.800]   All you can do is decompose churn into buckets but know nothing of propensities.
[00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:26.720]   For example, if you noticed that in one country a surprising 89% of churn came from customers
[00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.920]   with blue eyes, you might be prompted to launch an immediate inquiry into how your product
[00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:33.960]   UI somehow fails for blue-eyed customers.
