[00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.360]   the "why are there so many frequent flyers on airplanes" effect. At first blush, you'd think
[00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.720]   that if ultra-frequent flyers for example United 1000 represent the top 1%, then a 300 person
[00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.160]   flight might have 3 or 4 on board while in reality it's more like 20-30. But that's it. Frequent
[00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.240]   flyers are overrepresented on airplanes because they fly more, just like one-year contracts are
[00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:30.720]   overrepresented in renewals because they renew more. Let's look at an example. We have a company
[00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:35.840]   that signs 1-year, 2-year, and 3-year deals. Let's assume customers renew for the same duration as
[00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.320]   their initial contract, so there is no actual confidence reduction in play. Every deal is
[00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:46.800]   $100,000 in annual recurring revenue, ARR. We'll calculate ACD on an ARR-weighted basis. Let's
