[00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:32.880]   Well, that sounds dandy until you think for a minute about survivor bias, the often inadvertent
[00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.240]   logical error in analyzing data from only the survivors of a given experiment or situation.
[00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.200]   Survivor bias is subtle, but here are some common examples.
[00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.080]   I first encountered survivor bias in mutual funds when I realized that look-back studies
[00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:50.120]   of prior 5 or 10-year performance include only the funds still in existence today.
[00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.280]   If you eliminate my bogeys, I'm actually an below-par golfer.
[00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.680]   My favorite example is during World War II.
[00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.320]   Scientists examined the pattern of anti-aircraft fire on returning bombers and argued to strengthen
[00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.520]   them in the places that were most often hit.
[00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:02.640]   This was exactly wrong.
[00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.520]   The places where returning bombers were hit were already strong enough.
[00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.600]   You needed to reinforce them in the places that the downed bombers were hit.
[00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.880]   You needed to reinforce them in the places that the downed bombers were hit.
[00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.360]   NES books are plagued with survivor bias.
[00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:18.600]   Among other classics, both good to great and built to last fall victim to it.
[00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.680]   So let's turn back to churn rates.
[00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.360]   If you're going to calculate an overall expansion or retention rate, which way should
