[00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.080]   Number two is the obvious answer.
[00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.000]   You should include the ARR from customers who choose to stop being customers in calculating
[00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.760]   an overall churn or expansion rate.
[00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:48.320]   Calculating it the first way can be misleading because you are looking at the ARR expansion
[00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.080]   only from customers who choose to continue being customers.
[00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.680]   Let's make this real via an example.
[00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.680]   The ARR today is contained in the boxed area.
[00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:00.600]   The survivor bias question comes down to whether you include or exclude the orange rose from
[00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:01.880]   year ago.
[00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:02.880]   ARR.
[00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.640]   The difference can be profound.
[00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:09.120]   In this simple example, the survivor biased expansion rate is a nice 101%.
[00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.020]   However the non-biased rate is only 71% which will get you a quick "don't let the door
