[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.560]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from May 14th, 2015 titled "Simplifiers
[00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.160]   Go Far, Complexifiers Get Stuck."
[00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.320]   There are two types of people in business, simplifiers, who make complex things simple.
[00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.360]   Complexifiers, who make simple things complex.
[00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.560]   Quick joke, what does a complexifier call a simplifier?
[00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.080]   Answer, boss.
[00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.160]   Somewhere, somehow, some people decided that in business you need to make everything complicated
[00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:25.920]   and speak using business jargon.
[00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.320]   Well, that's an interesting proposal, and I'm not necessarily opposed to it, so let
[00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:33.320]   me run it up the flagpole so we can kick it around as a strawman.
[00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:37.080]   Since I hear the idea has some traction in the field, let me reach out to the guys upstairs
[00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.880]   and we'll see if we have the bandwidth to go forward with this.
[00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:44.040]   If the cost is north of $100,000, I may have to backburner it, because we need to keep
[00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.640]   some dry powder pending the results of the strategy meeting, where I know we're considering
[00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:48.680]   a pivot.
[00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:52.480]   Right now, the long pole in the tent isn't marketing, but strategy, so let's keep lines
[00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:53.480]   open.
[00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:57.480]   Okay, oodos to the team for coming up with such a great value proposition, but for now
[00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.440]   I'm afraid can't lean in on this one.
[00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.640]   That's one way of hiding behind complexity, making yourself flat and comprehensible.
[00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:07.080]   While that may impress your peers, your subordinates will mock you and your superiors will ask
[00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.640]   to speak to someone else.
[00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.360]   As I argued in this post, when dealing with senior people you need to speak clearly and,
[00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.160]   above all, answer the question.
[00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.040]   In most organizations, while jargon and doublespeak may be prevalent in middle management, they
[00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:19.900]   are nearly absent in the boardroom.
[00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:24.220]   The other way of hiding behind complexity is not linguistic but conceptual, finding an
[00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:28.320]   upstream or bigger picture issue that will block progress at the lower level.
[00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.320]   Consider this statement.
[00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:33.200]   I'd like to cut over to the new process, but we haven't completed the training yet.
[00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.840]   Is this, as it appears, a valid reason for not making progress on moving to the new process,
[00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.160]   or is it disguised passive resistance?
[00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:43.780]   For example, I don't want to move to the new process so I keep having trouble scheduling
[00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:44.900]   the training.
[00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.200]   Or is it bonafide complexification?
[00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.720]   If the training can be boiled down to a single page that everyone can read in five minutes
[00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:52.240]   then just cut over.
[00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.680]   Remember the old saw?
[00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.920]   When you ask the time, some people will tell you how to build a watch.
[00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.120]   Others will tell you how to build a Swiss village.
[00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:02.640]   My test for spotting complexifiers is look for the following pattern.
[00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.280]   Slow progress on results.
[00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:08.880]   Blamed on everything being difficult or complicated, with a tendency to find artificial prerequisites
[00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.040]   that sound plausible, but on further examination aren't.
[00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.240]   Things are as complex as we want to make them.
[00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:18.000]   Most of the time, complexity is an excuse for either not wanting or not knowing how
[00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.200]   to do something.
[00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.200]   My advice?
[00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:25.120]   Strive to make things simple, seek to understand them, struggle to find apt metaphors for them.
[00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.640]   If you're not burning energy trying to simplify things for your audience, you are most likely
[00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.000]   a complexifier.
[00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.560]   If so, the next time you're about to explain to someone why something takes so long is so
[00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:38.740]   complicated or requires five steps to be completed before starting, ask yourself, "Do I really
[00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:42.280]   believe this or am I making it complicated because I either don't want or don't know
