[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.160]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from January 27, 2016, titled "How to Run a Sales Kickoff."
[00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:11.720]   Since we're wrapping up what has been a simply amazing Host Analytics 2016 Sales Kickoff,
[00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:17.260]   I thought I'd share some of the rules I've developed, learned, appropriated, discovered, etc. during my career.
[00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:21.860]   Rule 1. For salespeople, your signed compensation plan is your admission ticket to kick off.
[00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:25.600]   This is incredibly important because comp plans define what you want your salespeople to do
[00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.600]   and too many companies don't get them finalized early enough in the quarter,
[00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.600]   leaving sales in a directionless limbo for weeks or months.
[00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.900]   Rule 2. The purpose of the kick off is to send sales reps home from the event fired up
[00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.200]   and having everything they need to be successful in the new year.
[00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.200]   When they get on the plane home, they should know their territory, their compensation plan,
[00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.600]   all the new messaging, all the latest competitive information, the new pricing,
[00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.400]   and have all the new kit required for their success.
[00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.400]   Rule 3. Be inclusive.
[00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.400]   At the companies I've run, we follow a simple philosophy.
[00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.700]   The event is a sales kick off to which the whole company is invited,
[00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:02.700]   so don't complain if the content is too rara or too salesy because it can't be.
[00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.100]   It's a sales kick off.
[00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:09.600]   But invite everyone so they can benefit both from the communications of plans, goals, and changes for the new year,
[00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.400]   and also from the contagious enthusiasm of hanging out with the sales force at a rara event.
[00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.900]   Rule 4. Mix it up.
[00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.900]   Don't run all day on a single-track, keynote-only format.
[00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.200]   Yes, do some keynotes.
[00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.600]   We'll have track sessions as well.
[00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.200]   Mix in some panel discussions.
[00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.800]   A game or contest is always fun, particularly if you take the trouble to ensure it's on message.
[00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:31.800]   Ideally, let people choose freely about which track sessions they attend and which they don't.
[00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.700]   Rule 5. Invite some customers.
[00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:38.100]   There's nothing like a customer panel to communicate the reality of the product market back to the organization.
[00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:41.500]   They're usually honored to come and their comments make a big impact to the organization.
[00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.500]   They're usually honored to come and their comments make a big impact.
[00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.700]   Rule 6. Remember EMDI as the four major things to do at a kickoff.
[00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.600]   Educate/motivate/decorate/enebriate.
[00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.700]   For decoration, have an awards dinner where you recognize achievement across the organization.
[00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:59.300]   On inebriation, remind employees to do so within bounds.
[00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:03.300]   At almost every kickoff I've been to, there's been at least one person who takes it far.
[00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.900]   My favorite story was a sales rep who urinated on a roulette table at a Vegas kickoff
[00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:11.400]   and who, subsequently barred from the casino, was unable to traverse it to gain access to the ballroom
[00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.900]   and fired from the casino, was unable to traverse it to do at a kickoff.
[00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:15.700]   Session.
[00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:19.200]   Rule 7. Have an open mic executive Q&A.
[00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.200]   These can be awkward and some CEOs hate doing them,
[00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:27.700]   but in a healthy organization you should be able to put the exec team in front of the company to answer questions.
[00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:30.400]   Rule 8. Invite analysts as speakers.
[00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.200]   You get a double win when you do this.
[00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:38.300]   You get to hear what the analyze has to say and the analysts get to see all the many happy smiling faces in your company
[00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.100]   and how much it has grown since the last time they were by.
[00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.400]   Rule 9. Have some silly time.
[00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:48.100]   Do things to break down hierarchical barriers and make the CEO and execs more approachable.
[00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.500]   Costumes, videos, etc. can go a long way in this department.
[00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.700]   Rule 10. Make it better every year.
[00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:58.100]   This is hard, but we did successfully both at MarkLogic and at Host Analytics.
[00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:02.700]   Always add some new twist, some new event, some new thing, some increased production values,
[00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:04.900]   a better guest speaker, something every year.
[00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:06.000]   Bonus rule 11.
[00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.500]   Work with a top quality events person and a top quality production company to execute it.
[00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.400]   This means zero time gets wasted on production related problems
