[00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.960]   clowns anymore. They're your clowns. You own them. The second thing you must realize is how
[00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.920]   quickly that will occur. Typically, I'd say it takes about 90 days before the organization.
[00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.800]   For example, your boss, your peers, perceives the team as your team. That's not a long time,
[00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.840]   so you need to use it well. A key part of any new executive's job is not just to assess the
[00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:53.440]   business situation, but also to assess his or her team. You may have inherited some great people
[00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.000]   and some weak ones. You might have great people who are in the wrong roles. You may have some great
[00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.080]   people who are beaten down and need to be uplifted. You may even have some people who
[00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:05.840]   really need to go pursue that career in real estate that they've always wanted.
[00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:09.920]   Whether you've inherited the bad news bears, the A-Team fool, or something in between,
[00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.160]   you don't have a lot of time before that team becomes your team. So, what should you do about
[00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.120]   it? Invest a lot of your early time in understanding your team, their strengths and their weaknesses,
[00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.680]   what their internal customers think of them, what you think of their work, what co-workers think.
[00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.160]   Understand their backgrounds, interview them, and go review their LinkedIn profiles or CVs.
[00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.880]   Remember that it's not black and white. It's not as simple as "good person" versus "bad person."
[00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.880]   Oftentimes, it's about the role, "Is that person a great product manager who's over his head in a
[00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.720]   director role? Is that person a great customer success person, but she's currently struggling
[00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:46.000]   with a direct sales job?" Or, remember that it's about the climate. Maybe the team is a bunch of
[00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.640]   great people who are just scaling down. Or maybe they're good people, on fire and already performing
[00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.920]   at 98% of their potential. The climate can turn stars into dogs, and vice versa, so you need to
[00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.560]   figure out who's sailing into a headwind and who's benefiting from a tailwind. Remember that it's
[00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.800]   about direction. If the team executed a bad strategy really well and failed, that's quite
[00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.520]   different from executing a great strategy poorly. To what extent was the team aimed well or aimed
[00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.320]   poorly in terms of direction? Remember that. It's about personal wants and needs. Where
[00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.160]   do your team members want to be in a few years? Do they see a way to get there from here at your
[00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:22.320]   company? Are they happy with short-term constraints? Or are they struggling to get out of meetings in
[00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.720]   time to hit childcare before those draconian fines kick in? Once you've gathered that data,
[00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.880]   then sit down with your manager, deliver the assessment, and make a proposal. Because after
[00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.360]   about 90 days, it's not the team anymore. It's your team. So you better focus on having the right
