[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from July 9, 2016 titled, "You Can Never Fire
[00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.160]   Someone Too Early." The first time I heard the VC adage, "You Can Never Fire Someone Too Early,"
[00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.880]   it rubbed me the wrong way. It sounded harsh and unfeeling. It seemed flippant. It felt trite. It
[00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.040]   seemed, frankly, like one of those things people say in a press box but never on the playing field.
[00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.600]   But slowly, as with most VC adages, I found the truth in it. Once you dismiss the initial
[00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.800]   tendency to rebuff it for its harshness and try to really understand what it means,
[00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.040]   it's hard to disagree with. So what does it mean? As an executive, by the time you find out there's
[00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:35.360]   a problem, there has already been considerable damage done and you need to fix it right away to
[00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:40.000]   prevent more damage. As an executive, there will always be a time lag between when co-workers know
[00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.480]   there is a problem and you learn there is one. Respect for hierarchy and politesse are just two
[00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:50.160]   things that delay signal transmission. Rationalization, concept avoidance, and denial are three others.
[00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.080]   As the hiring manager, you will tend to rationalize away problems because you hired the person.
[00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.760]   Firing them would show a concrete mistake on your part and pew,
[00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:02.480]   to you in the position of having to make every hire. Deep down, you are rooting for individual
[00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.720]   X to succeed and that biases your perspective and delays your decision. You want a team of
[00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.880]   stars and superstars. If you are even considering terminating someone, it means,
[00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.760]   definitionally, they are not a star or superstar. Ergo, you should not want them on your team.
[00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.120]   This is a tough one to internalize, but it's true. Harsh as it may sound,
[00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.880]   the mere act of questioning whether you should terminate someone means that you probably should.
[00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:25.760]   People who have known about the problem longer than you have are weighty.
[00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.400]   Eng and watching. How long will you tolerate the behavior? What does that mean about standards of
[00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.560]   competence you set? How many subordinates will respond to recruiter calls while you figure this
[00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.800]   out? And because your learning of the problem is definitionally phase lagged, people may have been
[00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.280]   waiting quite a while. You may have lost some already. Empirically, when you ask seasoned
[00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.360]   managers about this topic, virtually everyone says that they never fired someone too early,
[00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.720]   but have often done so too late. Hire slow, fire fast as the other hiring adage goes.
