[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.920]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from August 22nd, 2016 titled "The Three Golden
[00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.480]   Rules of Feedback." I was speaking to my executive coach the other day and we had a great conversation
[00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.960]   about feedback. I'm going to adapt what she said into some pithy advice from managers on how to
[00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:20.800]   give feedback. Here are the three golden rules of feedback. It has to be honest. It has to be kind.
[00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:25.680]   It has to be timely. Honest feedback. When you give someone feedback, it has to be authentic.
[00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.680]   It has to be what you really feel. It can't be candy-coated. It has to be what you honestly
[00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.000]   feel about the situation, tempered by the humility that you may not necessarily be right,
[00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.120]   or that right and wrong may not even have meaning in a given situation.
