[00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.760]   They've been written by our competitor. That's probably just coincidence.
[00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:34.320]   These things, all of them, are bad news.
[00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.120]   Because many people's first reaction to bad news is denial,
[00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:40.880]   the great thing about the first signed rule is that you remove discretion from the equation.
[00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.280]   We don't want you to wait until you are sure there is trouble.
[00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.800]   Then it's probably too late. We want you to ask for help at the first sign.
[00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.800]   The rule doesn't just apply to sales.
[00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.440]   The same principle applies to pretty much everything.
[00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.400]   Strategic partnerships, for example. They've gone quiet.
[00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.320]   Analyst relations, for example.
[00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:59.280]   It feels like the agenda is set for enemy A product development.
[00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.120]   For example, I'm worried we've badly overscoped this. Financing, for example.
[00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.120]   They're not calling back after the partner meeting.
[00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.560]   Recruiting, for example. The top candidates seem to be leaning back.
[00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:12.080]   HR, for example. Our top salesperson hated the new comp plan.
[00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:15.600]   I'll always thank Larry for sharing this nugget of wisdom and many others with me.
[00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:19.280]   And I'll always thank Larry for sharing this nugget of wisdom and many others with me.
[00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.000]   And I'll always advise every manager I know to follow it.
