[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.840]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from September 15, 2016, titled "The Era of
[00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:10.240]   Consumer Deception – Why Do We Tolerate Such Price Opacity?" I was wondering the other day
[00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.800]   why Southwest would spend millions of dollars to remind people that bags fly free. I'd argue there
[00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:20.080]   are two reasons. It generally supports their friendly and transparent low-fies brand strategy.
[00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:25.680]   It reminds customers that a $500 fare on United might actually cost you more than a $550 on
[00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.480]   Southwest if you've got a few bags. Prices have become so opaque over the past few decades that
[00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.800]   not only are consumers routinely surprised when they receive a bill, but companies now feel
[00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.680]   compelled to spend millions to remind them that quoted prices are often "Apple/Orange's"
[00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.720]   comparisons. It's not hard to find examples of price opacity. Mortgages with variable rate
[00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:48.880]   structures people don't understand and which exposes them to massive increase in payments,
[00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:54.400]   that is, the 2008 crisis. Bank accounts that have no monthly fee, but are laden with subtle and not
[00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:59.520]   inexpensive fees that seem to silently sneak back in as terms are quietly changed. Numerous airline
[00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.720]   refundability tiers change fee policies, per-seat premium economy seat fees, and baggage fees that
[00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:09.520]   make true price comparison next to impossible. Rental car policies like Hertz's usurious
[00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:14.320]   $10/gallon refueling fee or the maze of overpriced and often unneeded insurance
[00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.320]   see options that can double the price of a rental. Teaser rates for many services,
[00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:22.960]   including cellular and internet, that bear no resemblance to the actual monthly fees.
[00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:27.360]   Most, but not all, of the time I manage to sidestep these problems because I'm sophisticated and
[00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.920]   can figure them out when I take the time, because I carry balances that preclude most of the sneaky
[00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.640]   banking fees, and because I fly a lot and get exempt from some of the change fees and seat fees.
[00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:40.480]   But just the other day, while I was in the midst of congratulating myself for avoiding the Hertz
[00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.520]   $10/gallon refueling fee, I looked on the receipt and saw a per-mile fee that nearly doubled the
[00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:49.840]   cost of my rental. When was the last time a rental car didn't have unlimited miles?
[00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:53.200]   It's a cat and mouse game and companies keep getting better at playing it.
[00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:56.800]   Now you could argue that this opacity is a company's way of fighting back against price
[00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.920]   competition and particularly the price transparency and comparability that the internet brought.
[00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.000]   In an era of price comparison engines that scour the internet for the best deal,
[00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.720]   why not sneak in some fees that give you an edge? You can argue, as people often do when it comes
[00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:15.040]   to the airlines, that we've done it to ourselves. Our consumer behavior has trained the companies
[00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.840]   towards these strategies, and that may be true, but we need to accept that these strategies are often
[00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:24.400]   fundamentally dishonest. I realized this as my kids got older and I had to explain how rental
[00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:28.960]   cars work, which I still don't know that well, apparently, how airfares work, self-insure against
[00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.560]   cancellation by throwing out a ticket every now and then as opposed to getting gouged on
[00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:37.760]   refundable fares or just fly southwest, how credit cards work, that's a long one, how mortgages work,
[00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:42.720]   and on and on. It's what in Texas they call a "boiled fraud problem." It's happened so slowly
[00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.240]   and incrementally that we've just gotten accustomed to it. And the people most hurt
[00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:51.120]   by the practices tend to be at the bottom of the socioeconomic ladder, for example, payday loans,
[00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.000]   and have the least voice. And this society of deception already extends well beyond consumer
[00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:02.800]   pricing. Contests and prizes are another huge area, like fake $1 million TV show prizes,
[00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:07.760]   for example, America's Got Talent, that are actually a 40-year annuity worth more like
[00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:13.280]   $300,000, fake unwinnable TV contests like American Ninja Warrior, which has only been
[00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:18.080]   completed twice, which are made harder every year so nobody wins the fake 40-year 1 million dollar
[00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.720]   annuity, or even state lotteries, which started the annuity deception, which typically pay out
[00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:28.480]   over 20 years, slashing prize values by about half. But where we've ended up is not acceptable.
[00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.120]   Ironically, after the internet brought a brief wave of price transparency, we have ended with
[00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.400]   potentially more opacity than we had before as fees and terms and packaging get ever more complex.
[00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.160]   We're eroding consumer trust by living in an era of manufactured confusion and price
[00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.640]   deception. You may not think this is a big deal, but I'd argue it's like Malcolm Gladwell's
[00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:51.200]   broken window theory. If we tolerate constant small deceptions in our lives, we open the doors
[00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:57.280]   to the big ones. This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI with permission from Dave.
