[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.760]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from June 10th, 2017, titled "Blocking the end run, 11 words to reduce politics in your organization."
[00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:13.660]   People are people. Sometimes they're conflict-diverse and just not comfortable saying certain things to their peers.
[00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.700]   Sometimes they don't like them and are actively trying to undermine them.
[00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:20.560]   Sometimes they're in a completely functional relationship, but have been too darn busy to talk.
[00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.920]   So when this happens, how do you, as a manager, respond? What should you do?
[00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:29.020]   "Hey Dave, I wanted to say that Sarah's folks really messed up on the Acme call this morning.
[00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.760]   They weren't ready with the proposal, and were completely not in line with my sales team."
[00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.100]   Do you pile on?
[00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:38.500]   "Again, Sarah's folks are out of control. I'm going to go blaster," the young Dave responds.
[00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.960]   Do you investigate? Three, do you defend?
[00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:44.520]   Well, that doesn't sound like Sarah. Her team's usually buttoned up.
[00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.460]   In the first case, you're going off half-cocked without sufficient information, which,
[00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:53.960]   while emotionally satisfying in the short term, often leads to a mess followed by several apologies in the midterm.
[00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:59.840]   In the second case, you're being manipulated into investigating something when perhaps you were planning a better use of your time that day.
[00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.440]   In the third case, you're going off half-cocked again, but in the other direction.
[00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.620]   In all three cases, you're getting sucked into politics.
[00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:08.780]   Politics? Is it really politics?
[00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:13.960]   Well, how do you think Sarah is going to feel in when you show up asking a dozen questions about the Acme call?
[00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:19.660]   She'll certainly consider it politics, and, among other things, there's about a 98% chance that she will say,
[00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:22.140]   "Gosh, I wish Bill came and talked to me first."
[00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.740]   At which point, if you're like me, you're going to say, "No, no, no, no. I know what you're thinking.
[00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:30.500]   Don't worry, this isn't political. It's not like Bill was avoiding you on this one.
[00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:35.540]   He just happened to be talking to me about another issue, and he brought this up at the end. It's not political, no."
[00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:40.040]   But can you be sure? Maybe it just did pop into Bill's mind during the last minute of the other call.
[00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:44.500]   Or maybe it didn't. Maybe the reason Bill called you was a masterfully political pretext.
[00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:45.920]   Can you know the difference?
[00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:49.740]   So what do you say to Bill when he drops the comment about Sarah's team into your call?
[00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:55.040]   The 11 words that reduce politics in any organization, what did Sarah say when you talked to her about this?
[00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:56.240]   Mic drop.
[00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:58.900]   Props to Martin Cook for teaching me the 11 words.
