[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.340]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from January 31, 2018 titled, "How to Walk from a Deal."
[00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:10.800]   Like it or not, once in a while it's appropriate for a vendor to walk away from a prospective deal.
[00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.260]   Why might you want to do that?
[00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:15.100]   You think your product is a poor fit with the customer's needs.
[00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.640]   You believe there is insufficient budget to achieve success on the project.
[00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.060]   You feel like the deal is wired for another vendor.
[00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.480]   That is, you think you are Colin Fodder in the evaluation process.
[00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.700]   You and all your fellow reps are fully booked with other, more qualified opportunities.
[00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:33.040]   One day, I should probably write a post on how to make the critical stay versus walk decision.
[00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.920]   But today, I want to focus on something downstream of that.
[00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:41.580]   I want to focus on how to successfully walk from a deal once you've decided that it's necessary to do so.
[00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:45.260]   A good walk away process should pass three tests in the mind of the customer.
[00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.760]   The customer should feel like they were treated respectfully.
[00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.140]   In the future, the customer should remain interested in buying from both you individually and your company,
[00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.640]   should circumstances be different.
[00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.980]   Ideally, they will be more interested in buying from you because you walked.
[00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.900]   The customer should feel like the decision was not unilateral.
[00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.820]   Given these three tests, here are a few ways not to walk away from an opportunity.
[00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:07.120]   Calling five minutes before a meeting to say you're too busy to work on the opportunity
[00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:09.160]   because you don't think it's qualified anyway.
[00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:13.460]   Leaving a voicemail in the middle of the night saying that you've decided to stop pursuing the opportunity.
[00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:19.120]   Telling the customer their problem is too simple and/or their people are not sufficiently sophisticated to use your software.
[00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:24.220]   Emailing to say that they are running a ridge process in which you can no longer, in good conscience, compete.
[00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:25.300]   And there are lots more.
[00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:28.680]   In short, there are a lot of w-wrong ways to walk from an opportunity.
[00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.060]   The right way involves due.
[00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.560]   Aying the following things.
[00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:32.640]   Bring it up quickly.
[00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.140]   Once you realize there's good reason to walk, you immediately get in touch with the customer.
[00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:40.900]   Get the key contact on the phone and saying you're considering dropping out and would welcome the chance to explain why.
[00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:45.360]   Have a meeting or call to discuss the reasons you believe you should no longer participate in the sales cycle.
[00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.200]   Ask for their feedback on those reasons.
[00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.080]   Unless you hear otherwise in their feedback, thank them for their time.
[00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:53.620]   Check back in later, for example, in a few months to ask how things turned out.
[00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:56.660]   Amazingly, a lot of salespeople are afraid to walk away core.
[00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:57.540]   Directly.
[00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:02.580]   So they procrastinate and then, suddenly, at the 11th hour, burst out saying, "We're not coming."
[00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:09.980]   This leaves a terrible impression on the customer and denies them the chance to correct potential misunderstandings in the logic that led to the walk away decision.
[00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:12.900]   My company has won deals by walking away in the right fashion.
[00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.140]   To be clear, I am not advocating bluffing.
[00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.500]   When you say you're walking, you need to be prepared to do so.
[00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:25.220]   But I have seen cases where the walk away attempt revealed either a misunderstanding of the problem or the fact that no other vendor was willing to tell the customer what they didn't want to hear.
[00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:29.220]   I've seen cases where we get invited back 6 to 18 months later and then win the deal.
[00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.180]   I've also seen cases where the rep mingles the walk away process.
[00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:40.220]   The customer goes ballistic and I, as CEO, need to jump in, eat a large piece of humble pie, figure out what's going on, and assign a new rep to the deal.
[00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:41.860]   We've won a few of these as well.
[00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:47.020]   A fair number of salespeople like to brag about walking from deals, yet relatively few are mindful in how they do it.
[00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:53.140]   Those who are mindful and who follow the rules and steps above will sell more in both the short and long term than those who are not.
[00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:58.060]   This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI with permission from Dave.
