[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.240]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from October 4, 2018, titled "Lost and Founder,
[00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.200]   a Painful Yet Valuable Read." In an industry obsessed with the 1 in 10,000 decacorns and the
[00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:15.680]   stories of high-flying startups and their larger-than-life founders, Lost and Founder takes a real
[00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.720]   look at what it's like to found, fund, work at, and build a quite successful but not media,
[00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.840]   and Sandhill Road worships startup. Rand Fishkin, the founder of Moz, tells the story of his company
[00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.480]   from its founding as a mother/son website consultancy in 2001 until his handing over
[00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:36.320]   the reins in the midst of battling depression to a new CEO in February 2014. But you don't read
[00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.640]   Lost and Founder to learn about Moz, you read it to learn about Rand and the lessons he learned along
[00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:47.920]   the way. Excerpt. By 2017, my company, Moz, was a $45 million-year venture-backed B2B software
[00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.240]   provider, creating products for professionals who helped their clients or teams with Search Engine
[00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:58.160]   Optimization, SEO. In layman's terms, we make software for marketers. They use our tools to help
[00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.400]   websites rank well in Google's search engine, and as Google became one of the world's richest,
[00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.600]   most influential companies, our software rose to high demand. Moz is neither an overnight,
[00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.480]   billion-dollar success story nor a tragic tale of failure. The technology and business press
[00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:16.960]   tend to cover companies on one side or the other of this pendulum, but it's my belief that, for
[00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.200]   the majority of entrepreneurs and teams, there's a great deal to be learned from the highs and lows
[00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:26.080]   of a more middle-of-the-road startup lifecycle. Fishkin's style is transparent and humble. While
[00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:30.720]   the book tells a personal tale, it is laden with important lessons. In particular, I love his views
[00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:36.880]   on Pivots, Chapter 4. While it's a hipword, the reality is that pivoting, while sometimes required
[00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.440]   and which sometimes results in an amazing second effort, means that you have failed at your primary
[00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:46.720]   strategy. While I'm a big believer in a merchant strategy, few people discuss pivots as honestly as
[00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:52.080]   Fishkin. Fundraising, chapters 6 and 7. He does a great job explaining venture capital from the VC
[00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.120]   perspective, which then makes his conclusions both logical and clear. His advice here is invaluable.
[00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:02.960]   Every founder who's unfamiliar with VC 101 should read this section. Making Money, Chapter 8, and
[00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:07.840]   The Economics of Founding or Working at a Startup. His somewhat contrarian thoughts on the minimum
[00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:14.160]   viable product, MVP concept, Chapter 12. I think in brand new markets, MVPs are fine. If you've never
[00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.160]   seen a car, then you're not going to look for windows, leather seats, or cup holders. But in
[00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.200]   more established markets, Fishkin has a point. The exceptional viable product, EVP, is probably a
[00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.920]   better concept. His very honest thoughts on when to sell a startup, Chapter 13, which reveal the
[00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:32.960]   inherent interest conflicts between founders, feces, and employees. His cheat codes for next time,
[00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:38.720]   Tase Yavdor. D. Finally, in a Silicon Valley where failure is supposedly a red badge of courage,
[00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.840]   but one only worn after your next big success, Fishkin has a unique take on vulnerability,
[00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.640]   Chapter 15, and his battles with depression. Detailed in this long, painful blog post which
[00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:53.360]   he wrote the night before this story from the book about a foundry CEO summit, near the start
[00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.920]   of the session, Brad asked all the CEOs in the room to raise their hand if they had experienced
[00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:02.720]   severe anxiety, depression, or other emotional or mental disorders during their tenure as CEO.
[00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:07.920]   Every hand in the room went up, save too. At that moment, a sense of relief washed over me,
[00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:13.040]   so powerful I almost cried in my chair. I thought I was alone, a frail, former CEO who'd lost his
[00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.880]   job because he couldn't handle the stress and pressure and caved into depression. But those
[00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:21.120]   hands in the air made me realize I was far from alone. I was in fact part of an overwhelming
[00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:26.240]   majority, at least among this group. That mental transition from loneliness and shame to appear
[00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.480]   among equals forever changed the way I thought about depression and the stigma around mental
[00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.880]   disorders. Overall, in a world of business books that are often pretty much the same,
[00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:41.680]   Lost and Founder is both quite different and worth reading. TMI? At times, yes. TLDR? No way.
[00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.360]   Thanks, Rand, for sharing.
