[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from January 16, 2019
[00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.360]   titled "Two Natural Reactions That Great Managers Suppress."
[00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.320]   Most employees tolerate their managers more than love them.
[00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.640]   According to a year-old survey in Forbes,
[00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.080]   only about 50% of employees say the boss values their opinion.
[00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.440]   Only 35% of employees feel inspired by their boss.
[00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.880]   Some 25% say they can do a better job than their boss does.
[00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.160]   Almost 20% say that their boss takes credit for their work.
[00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.120]   Given this, there should be no surprise that employee-manager relations
[00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:30.800]   sometimes flare up and that when they do,
[00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.160]   employees often feel uncomfortable bringing the problem to their manager.
[00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.440]   According to a different survey,
[00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.640]   68% of employees are afraid to complain about their boss,
[00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.640]   fearing retaliation for so doing.
[00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.320]   Great companies recognize these.
[00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.800]   Perhaps sad facts and try to manage around them.
[00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.280]   For example, when I ran host analytics,
[00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.960]   I would end virtually every piece of employee communications with the following,
[00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.760]   "If you have a problem with your boss and feel comfortable raising it with them,
[00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:54.880]   then please do so.
[00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:56.720]   If you are not comfortable raising it with your boss,
[00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.000]   then please tell someone.
[00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.040]   Talk to HR.
[00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.400]   Talk to your manager's manager.
[00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.000]   Talk to any e-staff member.
[00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:02.800]   Talk to me.
[00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:03.920]   Talk to our coach."
[00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.320]   I know that when employee-manager relations are the issue,
[00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.720]   it's often impossible to raise the problem with your boss,
[00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.320]   so please tell someone else.
[00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.320]   In addition, beyond setting that as a policy,
[00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.960]   you can use other mechanisms to detect these issues.
[00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.960]   Periodic, ideally anonymous, employee surveys do a great job of finding.
[00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.560]   Hotspots, where an entire team is having problems with its manager.
[00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.800]   We used Culture Amp for employee surveys,
[00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.680]   and it's slicing and dicing lit up hotspots right away.
[00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:32.720]   Open-ended questions and comment fields also often reveal troubles on a more individual basis.
[00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.000]   So it is just walking around and asking people how they're doing.
[00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.160]   The goal from the company's perspective is to surface these problems so they can be addressed.
[00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:43.600]   Some managers, however, often react in a way that defeats that intent.
[00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.240]   When a problem is surfaced via an indirect channel,
[00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.600]   many managers' first instinct is, say, two things to the employee.
[00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.080]   "Why didn't you bring this to me directly?
[00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.760]   Why didn't you bring this to me sooner?"
[00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:54.960]   Both are wrong.
[00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.040]   Both not so subtly blame the employee.
[00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:58.880]   The first indirectly calling them a coward,
[00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.000]   and the second indirectly accusing them of perpetuating the problem,
[00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.400]   because you can't fix an issue you don't know about.
[00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:08.880]   Both show that you care more about yourself and your reputation than you do about the employee.
[00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.120]   Banish them from your management vocabulary.
[00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:13.040]   Great managers don't react this way.
[00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.480]   They replace the above two reactions with two far superior ones.
[00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.400]   Thank you for raising the problem to someone.
[00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.120]   Please tell me more about the problem so we can work on it.
[00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.320]   Maybe three months in the future, once, and if the problem is clearly fixed,
[00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.000]   then the manager can safely say,
[00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.360]   "By the way, why didn't you feel comfortable raising that problem to me anyway?"
[00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.640]   In that context, the question will sound like genuine interest in the feedback.
[00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.800]   In the heat of the moment, all it sounds like is blame.
[00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.600]   Assume not, regardless of channel used,
[00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.640]   raising a working relationship issue is very hard for the employee
[00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.640]   and was probably preceded by some combination of sleepless nights and tears.
[00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.000]   So thank them for doing the difficult thing and raising the issue.
[00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.880]   Regardless of how and, respect their courage by jumping in immediately to learn more about it.
