[00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.000]   rate is 40 percentage points higher than its free cash flow margin.
[00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.800]   It's an attempt at devising a simple rule to help software companies with the complex
[00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.920]   question of how to balance growth and profitability.
[00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:46.360]   One past study showed that while Rule of 40 compliant software companies made up a little
[00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:50.260]   more than half of all public software companies that they captured more than half of all public
[00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:54.280]   software companies that they captured more than 80% of all public market cap.
[00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:58.360]   Let's take a look at Piper's chart which plots Rule of 40 score on the x-axis and enterprise
[00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.780]   value, EV, divided by revenue on the y-axis.
[00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:05.220]   It also plots a presumably least-squares-fit line through the data points.
[00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:10.440]   Source, PJC Analysis, and SAP Capital IQ as of December 31, 2018.
[00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.800]   Of note, less than half of all companies in this set are Rule of 40 compliant.
[00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.860]   The median Rule of 40 score was 31.7%.
[00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:21.220]   The median multiple for companies in the set was 6.6 times.
[00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:25.080]   The slope of the line is 12, meaning that for each 10 percentage points of Rule of 40
[00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:29.220]   score improvement, a company's revenue multiple increases by 1.2 times.
[00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:35.120]   R to the 2 is 0.42 which, if I recall correctly, means that the Rule of 40 score explains 42%
[00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:36.600]   of the variability of the data.
[00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.640]   So, while there's lots it doesn't explain, it's still a useful indicator.
[00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.720]   A few nerdier things of note, remember that the line is only valid in the data range presented.
[00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:49.940]   Since no companies had a negative Rule of 40 score, it would be invalid extrapolation
[00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:52.960]   to simply continue the line down and to the left.
[00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.760]   Early-stage startup executives often misapply these charts forgetting the selection bias
[00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.040]   within them.
[00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.440]   Every company on the chart did well enough at some point in terms of size and growth
[00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.680]   to become a public SaaS company.
[00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:09.080]   Just because LivePerson LPSN has a 4 times multiple within Rule of 40 score of 10% doesn't
[00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:14.600]   mean your $20 million startup with the same score is also worth $4.10, as LPSN is a much
[00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:20.120]   bigger company, roughly $250 million, and already cleared many hurdles to get there.
[00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:23.960]   The big question around the Rule of 40 is, when should companies start to target it?
[00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:30.560]   A superstar like Elastic had 76% growth and 8% FCF margin, so a Rule of 40 score of 84%
[00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.120]   at its spectacular IPO.
[00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:39.120]   However, Avalara had 26% growth and 28% FCF margin for a Rule of 40 score of 2%, and its
[00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.120]   IPO went fine.
[00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:42.120]   Ditto AnaPlan.
[00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.680]   I'll be doing some work in the next few months to try and get better data on Rule of 40 trajectory
[00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.140]   into an IPO.
[00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:51.240]   My instinct at this point is that many companies target Rule of 40 compliance too early, sacrifice
[00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:54.880]   growth in the process, and hurt their valuations because they fail to deliver high growth and
[00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.480]   don't get the assumed customer acquisition cost efficiencies built in the financial models
[00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:02.480]   which end up, as one friend called them, spreadsheet-induced hallucinations.
