[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.640]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from May 11, 2019 titled, "What's the cause of death in your churn reporting?"
[00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:15.440]   In looking at this issue across several companies, I've noticed a disturbing trend/mist opportunity in how many SaaS companies classify the reason for customer churn.
[00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:21.520]   Roughly speaking, if companies were hospitals, they'd too frequently be reporting the cause of death as "stopped breathing."
[00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.840]   Yes, the patient who died stopped breathing. The question is, why did they stop breathing?
[00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.880]   In churn speak, yes, the customer who churned issued a churn notice and chose not to renew.
[00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.680]   The question is, why did they choose not to renew?
[00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:36.240]   The question is, why did they choose not to renew?
[00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.560]   Many people have written great posts on reasons customers churn and how to prevent them.
[00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:47.360]   These reasons often look like hierarchies, uncontrollable, got acquired, went bankrupt corporate edict, new sponsor controllable,
[00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.560]   failed implementation, product functionality, product ease of use, oversold/poor fit.
[00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.840]   These hierarchies aren't a bad start, but they aren't good enough either.
[00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.040]   A new sponsor isn't an automatic death sentence for a SaaS product.
[00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.480]   He or she might be, however, if the team using it had a rough implementation and was only half satisfied with the product.
[00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:08.000]   Similarly, a failed implementation will certainly reduce the odds of renewal,
[00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.040]   but sometimes people do have the will to start over, and why did the implementation fail in the first place?
[00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.720]   Physicians have been in the churn business much longer than SaaS companies, and I think they've arrived at a superior system.
[00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.280]   Here's an excerpt from the CDC's Physicians Handbook on Medical Certification of Death.
[00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.960]   Not a publication, I'd add, linked to by most SaaS bloggers.
[00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.960]   For example, you might see, "The rule above spells it out quite clearly."
[00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:36.320]   Do not enter terminal events such as Respiratory Arrest 10 without showing the ideology.
[00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:39.680]   That is, stopped breathing, by itself, isn't good enough.
[00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.720]   Just like sent churn notice or decided not to renew.
[00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.720]   I have not built out a full taxonomy here.
[00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.920]   Classifying churn in this way remained a future item on my to-do list at the time we sold my last company.
[00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:56.720]   Nevertheless, while I know it's not easy, I believe that companies should start trying to find a way to richly encode churn reasons using this chain concept
[00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.000]   so as to not lose critical information in encoding their data.
[00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.800]   Otherwise, we risk believing that all our customers churned because they sent us a churn notice or some easily blamed uncontrollable event.
[00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:14.160]   As one example, churned due to new sponsor, due to failed implementation, due to partner problem, due to partner training,
[00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:22.320]   or another, churned due to corporate edict, due to M&A, due to product dissatisfaction, due to oversold, due to sales training.
[00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:27.280]   These aren't perfect, but I'm trying quickly to demonstrate the real complexity behind why customers churn.
[00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.600]   For example, happy customers might challenge a corporate edict issued after an acquisition,
[00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.400]   so you can't just blame the edict, you have to look more deeply.
[00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.920]   If you knew that the customer fought the edict and failed, you might stop the chain there.
[00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.280]   But if you knew they were never terribly happy with the system because they were over-promised
[00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:47.280]   capabilities at the system because they were over-promised capabilities at the start, then you should code that into the chain too.
